TP.register({id:"lp3-g8",lp:3,grade:"8",mode:"curriculum",checks:2,nextGrade:"high school",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="The Weigh-Off";const STORY=["For five months, Emeka had fed, watered, and shaded one pumpkin, and he had come to know its moods the way some people know a pet’s. Now it sat on a pallet in the back of the truck like a sleeping bear, orange and enormous.","At the fairgrounds, a forklift driver waved them into a line that crept toward the scales. Emeka’s cousin Noor, who had come along to help, ran her hand along the back of the pumpkin and stopped.","“Meka. Feel this.”","Near the stem, the skin gave under his thumb, faintly damp and warm, the way fruit feels when it has begun to turn. It was a soft spot, no bigger than a coin. The rule sheet was taped to every table: Any pumpkin with rot or a hole is weighed for display only. No ribbon. No prize.","“It’s tiny,” Noor whispered. “Roll that side toward the fence. The judges have ninety pumpkins to check. They’ll never see it.”","Emeka looked down the line, weighing what he saw against what he knew. Old Mr. Brandt was polishing a pumpkin half the size of his, rubbing it with a rag as if it were a trophy already. Mr. Brandt had entered for eleven years and had never placed higher than fourth.","“If we hide it and win,” Emeka said, “who comes in second?”","Noor followed his eyes. She opened her mouth, then closed it, and she did not answer.","The whole summer played back in his head: the hailstorm he had covered the vine for, the mornings with the watering can before school. All of it had shrunk to a spot the size of a coin, which seemed to grow larger the longer he stared at it. No fairy godmother was coming to turn this pumpkin into a coach.","When the judge reached them, Emeka pointed at the stem. “There’s a soft spot. Right here.”","The judge pressed it, nodded, and wrote something down. “Display only,” she said. Then she paused. “Most people make me find it myself.”","The scale read 842 pounds. It was the heaviest number of the day, and it did not count. Emeka took a picture of it anyway."].join("\n\n");const story=()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true);const TON_TITLE="How to Grow a One-Ton Pumpkin";const TON=["A grocery store pumpkin weighs about fifteen pounds. The winners at the biggest pumpkin weigh-offs now weigh more than two thousand pounds. Growers reach these weights through a set of choices, not luck.","The first choice is the seed. Nearly every giant comes from one variety, the Atlantic Giant, and growers trade seeds from past champions. A seed from a huge pumpkin does not guarantee another one, but it improves the odds.","The second choice is hard for beginners. A healthy plant may start several young pumpkins. The grower picks one and removes all the others. The plant then sends its water and sugar to a single fruit instead of sharing them.","The third choice is underground. Growers bury the long vines under a few inches of soil. Buried vines sprout extra roots, and more roots can take in more water. This matters because a pumpkin is about ninety percent water. In the middle of summer, a giant can gain more than thirty pounds in one day.","Fast growth creates its own risk. If the fruit swells faster than its skin can stretch, the skin splits, and a split pumpkin cannot win at most contests. To slow things down, some growers build a shade tent over the fruit and cut back on water after heavy rain.","By October, the largest pumpkins are no longer round. They sag under their own weight like a ball losing air. A forklift, not a wheelbarrow, carries them to the scale."].join("\n\n");const OPED_TITLE="Opinion: Change the Soft-Spot Rule";const OPED=["The county should drop the rule that sends any pumpkin with a soft spot to the display table.","A soft spot the size of a coin has nothing to do with how heavy a pumpkin is, and weight is what this contest measures. The rule also punishes the wrong people. This fall a grower lost a ribbon over a bruise he found and reported himself.","Some will say rot can spread to the other pumpkins at the fairgrounds. Fine. The judges could weigh those pumpkins last and send them straight home.","Besides, this rule has been on the sheet for eleven years, and the fair has never been bigger than it was this fall.","Growers deserve better."].join("\n\n");const fr=(a,b)=>`<sup>${a}</sup>⁄<sub>${b}</sub>`;const pw=(b,e)=>`${b}<sup>${e}</sup>`;const cards=a=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:14px">${a.map(x=>`<span style="display:inline-block;border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;background:var(--surface);padding:8px 20px;font-size:2rem;font-weight:700">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.3rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.35rem"><tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td>${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;const srcCard=(tag,head,body)=>`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.3rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`;const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.6rem">Research question: How do farms cut the paths for a corn maze?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A","“Mapping a Maze with GPS,” an article by Elena Ruiz. Farm Technology Review magazine. September 2025.","Most large corn mazes are cut early, when the corn is about a foot tall. A tractor with a GPS receiver follows a digital map and mows the paths. A design that once took a week to mark by hand can now be cut in one afternoon.")}${srcCard("Source B","A post on a hobby forum by a user named MazeFan88. No date.","I heard they wait until the corn is fully grown and then cut the paths with a helicopter. Pretty sure every farm does it that way.")}</div>`;function grid(o){const S=o.size||500,max=o.max||8,u=(S-68)/(2*max),X=v=>34+(v+max)*u,Y=v=>S-34-(v+max)*u;const svg=F.coordGrid({max,quad4:true,size:S,labelEvery:1,polys:o.polys,points:(o.labels||[]).map(p=>[p[0],p[1]])});const t=(o.labels||[]).map(p=>`<text x="${X(p[0])+(p[3]||0)}" y="${Y(p[1])+(p[4]||0)}" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${p[2]}</text>`).join("");return svg.replace(/<\/svg>\s*$/,t+"</svg>")}const MAZE=[[-6,2],[-2,2],[-2,6],[-4,6]];const mazeGrid=()=>grid({polys:[MAZE],labels:[[-6,2,"M",-14,12],[-2,2,"A",14,12],[-2,6,"Z",14,-12],[-4,6,"E",-14,-12]]});const TUV=[[2,3],[5,3],[2,7]],TUV2=[[2,-3],[5,-3],[2,-7]];const flagGrid=()=>grid({polys:[TUV,TUV2],labels:[[2,3,"T",-14,10],[5,3,"U",14,10],[2,7,"V",-14,-10],[2,-3,"T′",-14,-10],[5,-3,"U′",16,-10],[2,-7,"V′",-14,12]]});const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"The Soft Spot",short:"Cite It",parts:[{std:["RL.8.1"],dur:135,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence for what a story says and for inferences",html:story,qs:["Under the contest rules, what happens to a pumpkin with rot? Read me the exact words that prove it.","Which one is the strongest evidence that Emeka worked hard on this pumpkin? Pick A, B, or C and tell why. A: “orange and enormous.” B: “the hailstorm he had covered the vine for, the mornings with the watering can before school.” C: “The scale read 842 pounds.”","The story never says why Emeka tells the judge about the soft spot. Why do you think he does? Read me the words that most strongly support your inference.","How does the judge feel about what Emeka did? Read me the one sentence that most strongly supports your answer."],key:["It is weighed, but it cannot win. Reads “Any pumpkin with rot or a hole is weighed for display only. No ribbon. No prize.” (RL.8.1, explicit evidence)","B. It names things Emeka did for the pumpkin. A and C only tell how big the pumpkin is, and size alone does not prove hard work. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","He thinks it would be unfair to win by hiding the spot, because an honest grower would lose a place. Strongest evidence: “If we hide it and win, who comes in second?” May add that Mr. Brandt “had entered for eleven years and had never placed higher than fourth.” (RL.8.1, inference)","She respects him, or she is surprised and impressed. Strongest evidence: “Most people make me find it myself.” (RL.8.1, inference)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Number Patch",short:"Number Patch",parts:[{std:["8.NS.A.1"],dur:140,dir:"Answer number 1 out loud. Show your steps for the rest on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"sorting rational and irrational numbers and converting between fractions and repeating decimals",html:()=>cards(["−√81","√50","0.414141...","3π","1.75"]),qs:["Sort the five number cards into rational and irrational. Tell how you decided.","Divide to write "+fr(7,12)+" as a decimal. Which digit repeats?","Write 0.181818... (the 18 repeats) as a fraction.","Write 2.4666... (only the 6 repeats) as a fraction.","Use the same steps on 0.999... (the 9 repeats). What number do you get?"],key:["Rational: −√81, 0.414141..., and 1.75. Irrational: √50 and 3π. Reasons: −√81 = −9. 0.414141... repeats. 1.75 ends. 50 is not a perfect square, and π never ends or repeats. Partial: the five sorted right with no reasons is approaching, and so is one misplaced card with the reasoning sound. (8.NS.A.1)","0.58333... The 3 repeats. Long division: 70 ÷ 12 = 5 with 10 left over, 100 ÷ 12 = 8 with 4 left over, then 40 ÷ 12 = 3 with 4 left over, again and again. Or: 7/12 as 7 × 1/12, and 1/12 is 0.08333... Partial: the decimal right with no repeating digit named is approaching. (8.NS.A.1)","18/99 = 2/11. Steps: two digits repeat, so 100x = 18.1818..., subtract x to get 99x = 18, so x = 18/99 = 2/11. Accept 18/99. Or: name it from the pattern, since a two-digit repeat sits over 99. Partial: 18/99 not reduced still counts; the method is what is rated. (8.NS.A.1)","222/90 = 37/15, or 2 and 7/15. Steps: 10x = 24.666... and 100x = 246.666..., subtract to get 90x = 222, so x = 222/90 = 37/15. Accept 222/90 or 111/45. Or: split off the whole number first, 2 + 0.4666..., then work on the decimal alone. (8.NS.A.1)","1. Steps: x = 0.999..., 10x = 9.999..., subtract to get 9x = 9, so x = 1. So 0.999... is another way to write 1. Or: 1/3 = 0.333... so three of them make 0.999... and also make 1. Partial: 1 with no steps shown is approaching. (8.NS.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Maze Blueprint",short:"Blueprint",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],dur:190,dir:"Shape MAZE is one section of a corn maze design. Start from the original shape each time. Tell your teacher the new coordinates.",desc:"coordinates after a translation rule, a reflection, a rotation, a dilation, and a two-step move",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${mazeGrid()}</div>`,qs:["The designer uses the rule (x, y) → (x + 7, y − 5). Describe this move in words. Then give the new coordinates of M, A, Z, and E.","Reflect shape MAZE across the y-axis. What are the new coordinates? Which coordinate changed, x or y?","Rotate shape MAZE 90° clockwise about the origin. Where do A and E land?","Dilate shape MAZE by a scale factor of "+fr(1,2)+" from the origin. What are the new coordinates? What happens to the length of side MA?","Two moves: reflect shape MAZE across the x-axis, and then translate it 3 units right. Where do M and Z land?"],key:["A translation 7 units right and 5 units down. M′(1, −3), A′(5, −3), Z′(5, 1), E′(3, 1). Or: count the squares on the grid instead of using the rule. Partial: the words right with one or two points wrong is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","M′(6, 2), A′(2, 2), Z′(2, 6), E′(4, 6). The x-coordinates change sign. The y-coordinates stay the same. Partial: the four points right with the wrong coordinate named is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","A′(2, 2) and E′(6, 4). The rule is (x, y) → (y, −x). Whole shape: M′(2, 6), Z′(6, 2). Or: trace the shape and turn the paper a quarter turn, or call it a 270° counterclockwise turn. Partial: one of the two points right is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","M′(−3, 1), A′(−1, 1), Z′(−1, 3), E′(−2, 3). Multiply every coordinate by 1/2. Side MA goes from 4 units to 2 units, half as long. Partial: the four points right with nothing said about side MA is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","M lands at (−3, −2) and Z lands at (1, −6). Steps: M(−6, 2) → (−6, −2) → (−3, −2). Z(−2, 6) → (−2, −6) → (1, −6). Or: do the translation first and the reflection second, which lands in the same place here. Partial: one of the two points right is approaching. (8.G.A.3)"]}]},{id:4,title:"The Line That Tips It",short:"Dialogue",parts:[{std:["RL.8.3"],dur:105,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the line you are talking about.",desc:"explaining how lines of dialogue and events provoke a decision and reveal character",html:story,qs:["Noor says, “Roll that side toward the fence. The judges have ninety pumpkins to check. They’ll never see it.” How do these lines push the story forward? What choice do they put in front of Emeka?","Emeka asks, “If we hide it and win, who comes in second?” What does this line reveal about him?","Just before he decides, Emeka sees Mr. Brandt polishing his pumpkin. How does that moment lead to his decision?"],key:["Her lines give Emeka a way to hide the spot and maybe win. Now he has to choose between hiding it and telling the truth, and the rest of the story is about that choice. (RL.8.3, dialogue propels the action and provokes a decision)","He is fair and thinks about other people. He sees that winning by hiding the spot would take a place away from someone who earned it. (RL.8.3, dialogue reveals character)","It makes the cost of cheating real. Mr. Brandt has tried for eleven years and has never placed higher than fourth, and he would be pushed down a place. Seeing him leads Emeka to tell the judge. (RL.8.3, an incident provokes a decision)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Verbal Vines",short:"Verbals",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:105,dir:"A verbal is a verb form doing another job. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding gerunds, participles, and infinitives and telling what job each one does",qs:["“Growing a giant pumpkin takes patience.” Which word is the verbal? What kind is it, and what job does it do in the sentence?","“The judge, holding a clipboard, walked down the line.” Find the verbal and name its kind. Who or what does it describe?","“Emeka’s goal was to grow the heaviest pumpkin.” Find the verbal and name its kind.","“Covered with a tarp, the vine survived the hail.” Find the verbal and name its kind. What does it describe?","Same word, two jobs. A: “The sagging pumpkin needed a forklift.” B: “Sagging is a sign of great weight.” In which sentence is sagging a gerund? In which is it a participle?"],key:["Growing. Gerund. With its phrase, “Growing a giant pumpkin,” it is the subject of the sentence, so it acts as a noun. (L.8.1, verbals)","holding. Participle. It describes the judge. (L.8.1, verbals)","to grow. Infinitive. (L.8.1, verbals)","Covered. Participle. It describes the vine. (L.8.1, verbals)","B is the gerund: Sagging is the subject and acts as a noun. A is the participle: sagging describes the pumpkin. (L.8.1, verbals)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Power Rows",short:"Power Rows",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.1"],dur:125,dir:"Seeds are planted in rows, and rows are counted in powers. Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"using the properties of integer exponents, including negative exponents",qs:["Write "+pw(3,5)+" × "+pw(3,2)+" as a single power of 3.","Write "+pw(6,9)+" ÷ "+pw(6,7)+" as a single power of 6. Then find its value.","Write ("+pw(2,3)+")<sup>4</sup> as a single power of 2.","Simplify "+pw(5,3)+" × "+pw(5,"−5")+". Write your answer as a fraction with no negative exponent.","Find the error. A student wrote "+pw(4,6)+" ÷ "+pw(4,2)+" = "+pw(4,3)+". What went wrong? What is the correct power?","Simplify ("+pw(2,4)+" × "+pw(2,"−9")+") ÷ "+pw(2,"−2")+". Write your answer as a fraction."],key:["3<sup>7</sup>. Same base, so add the exponents: 5 + 2 = 7. Or: write out three 3s and two 3s and count seven. Partial: 3¹⁰ comes from multiplying the exponents and does not count. (8.EE.A.1)","6<sup>2</sup> = 36. Same base, so subtract the exponents: 9 − 7 = 2. Or: cancel seven 6s from the factor strings. Partial: the power right with the value wrong is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)","2<sup>12</sup>. Power of a power, so multiply the exponents: 3 × 4 = 12. Or: write (2³)(2³)(2³)(2³) and add the exponents. Partial: 2⁷ comes from adding instead of multiplying and does not count. (8.EE.A.1)","5<sup>−2</sup> = 1/5<sup>2</sup> = 1/25. Add the exponents: 3 + (−5) = −2. Or: 125 ÷ 3,125 = 1/25 worked out as numbers. (8.EE.A.1)","The student divided the exponents. To divide powers with the same base, subtract the exponents: 6 − 2 = 4. Correct: 4<sup>4</sup>, which is 256. Partial: the right power with no account of what the student did is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)","1/8. Steps: 4 + (−9) = −5, then −5 − (−2) = −3, so the answer is 2<sup>−3</sup> = 1/2<sup>3</sup> = 1/8. Or: write it as 2⁴ ÷ (2⁹ × 2⁻²) and work the bottom first. Partial: 2⁻³ left with a negative exponent is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Heavy Words",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.8.4"],dur:120,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"figurative language, word choice and tone, and an allusion in a story",html:story,qs:["The pumpkin sits on the truck “like a sleeping bear.” What does this comparison tell you about the pumpkin? How does Emeka seem to feel about it?","“All of it had shrunk to a spot the size of a coin.” His summer did not really shrink. What does this sentence mean? What feeling does it create?","“No fairy godmother was coming to turn this pumpkin into a coach.” In the old tale of Cinderella, a fairy godmother uses magic to turn a pumpkin into a coach. What is the narrator saying about Emeka’s problem?","The last sentence says Emeka took a picture “anyway.” What does that one word tell you about how he feels at the end?"],key:["The pumpkin is huge, heavy, and still, and it seems powerful. Emeka is proud of it, or amazed by it. (RL.8.4, figurative meaning)","Five months of work now depend on one tiny flaw, as if nothing else matters. It creates a feeling of disappointment, or of something being unfair. (RL.8.4, figurative meaning and tone)","No magic will fix this for him. Nobody is coming to rescue him, so he has to face the problem himself. (RL.8.4, allusion)","He is still proud of the pumpkin, and of what he did, even though the weight did not count. The ending feels bittersweet: sad and proud together. (RL.8.4, word choice and tone)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Voice and Mood Check",short:"Voice + Mood",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:125,dir:"Answer out loud. When you fix a sentence, say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"active and passive voice, verb moods, and fixing shifts in voice and mood",qs:["“The winning pumpkin was lifted by a forklift.” Is this sentence active or passive? Say it in the other voice.","Name the mood of each sentence. A: “Weigh the pumpkin now.” B: “Did the scale break?” C: “The scale reads 842 pounds.”","Fix the verb: “I wish the contest was tomorrow.”","Finish the sentence with would and a verb: “If the vine had more water, it ____ faster.” What mood is the verb you added?","Fix the shift in voice: “Noor loaded the truck, and the pumpkin was tied down by Emeka.”","Fix the shift in mood: “Check the stem for soft spots, and then you should weigh the pumpkin.”"],key:["Passive. Active: A forklift lifted the winning pumpkin. (L.8.1, active and passive voice)","A is imperative, a command. B is interrogative, a question. C is indicative, a statement of fact. All three are needed. (L.8.1, verb moods)","I wish the contest were tomorrow. A wish takes were. (L.8.1, subjunctive mood)","If the vine had more water, it would grow faster. Conditional mood. Accept another verb that fits, such as would spread. (L.8.1, conditional mood)","Noor loaded the truck, and Emeka tied down the pumpkin. Both verbs are now active. (L.8.1, shift in voice)","Check the stem for soft spots, and then weigh the pumpkin. Both verbs are now commands. (L.8.1, shift in mood)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Root Cellar",short:"Root Cellar",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.2"],dur:125,dir:"Farmers keep roots in the cellar. You keep square roots and cube roots. Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"square root and cube root equations, evaluating roots, and knowing that √2 is irrational",qs:["Find the value of √121 and of ∛8 (the cube root of 8).","Solve x<sup>2</sup> = 49. Give every solution.","Solve x<sup>3</sup> = 27. Why is there only one solution?","Solve x<sup>2</sup> = "+fr(9,25)+". Give every solution.","A seed crate is shaped like a cube. Its volume is 216 cubic inches. Write an equation for the length of one edge. Then solve it.","Solve x<sup>2</sup> = 2. Write the exact solutions. Can they be written as fractions?"],key:["√121 = 11 and ∛8 = 2. Partial: one of the two right is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","x = 7 and x = −7, or ±√49. Both are needed. Partial: x = 7 only leaves the negative root out and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","x = 3, or ∛27. A negative number cubed is negative: (−3)<sup>3</sup> = −27, so −3 does not work. (8.EE.A.2)","x = 3/5 and x = −3/5. √9 = 3 and √25 = 5. Or: 9/25 = 0.36 and √0.36 = 0.6, which is the same answer. Partial: 3/5 only leaves the negative root out and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","s<sup>3</sup> = 216, so s = ∛216 = 6 inches. Check: 6 × 6 × 6 = 216. Or: list the cubes to 216, or test 5 and 6. Partial: the equation written with no solution is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","x = √2 and x = −√2. No. √2 is irrational, so it cannot be written as a fraction and its decimal never ends or repeats. Partial: 1.41 is a decimal approximation, not an exact solution, and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)"]}]},{id:10,title:"The One-Ton Pumpkin",short:"One Ton",parts:[{std:["RI.8.1"],dur:135,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence from a science article for explicit answers and inferences",html:()=>F.passage(TON,TON_TITLE,true),qs:["Why does a grower remove all but one young pumpkin from the plant? Read me the exact words that prove it.","Is a seed from a champion pumpkin a sure thing? Read me the one sentence that most strongly supports your answer.","Claim: Burying the vines helps a pumpkin gain weight. Find the two sentences that, together, most strongly support this claim.","A week of heavy rain falls in August. Is that good news for a grower? Read me the words that most strongly support your inference."],key:["So all the food goes to one fruit. Reads “The plant then sends its water and sugar to a single fruit instead of sharing them.” (RI.8.1, explicit evidence)","No. “A seed from a huge pumpkin does not guarantee another one, but it improves the odds.” (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","“Buried vines sprout extra roots, and more roots can take in more water.” and “This matters because a pumpkin is about ninety percent water.” Both are needed: one tells what burying does, and the other tells why water adds weight. (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","Not really. It is a risk. Strongest evidence: “If the fruit swells faster than its skin can stretch, the skin splits, and a split pumpkin cannot win at most contests.” May add that growers “cut back on water after heavy rain.” (RI.8.1, inference)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Find the Move",short:"Find the Move",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],dur:160,dir:"The maze crew left clues in coordinates. Answer out loud. Use the grid for number 3.",desc:"naming transformations from rules and coordinates, finding a scale factor, and finding an error in a rotation",qs:["A rule on the blueprint says (x, y) → (−x, −y). Which transformation is this? Test it with the point (3, 1).","A scarecrow at S(−4, 6) is moved by a dilation from the origin. It lands at S′(−6, 9). What is the scale factor?","Look at the grid. Rae says triangle TUV just slid straight down to make triangle T′U′V′. Use the coordinates to show why she is wrong. Which transformation is it?","Find the error. Jo rotated the point (3, −5) 90° counterclockwise about the origin and wrote (−5, −3). What did Jo do wrong? What is the correct point?","A translation moves the point (2, −3) to (−1, 1). Where does the same translation move the point (0, 4)?"],fig2:()=>`<div class="figure">${flagGrid()}</div>`,key:["A rotation of 180° about the origin. Both coordinates change sign, so (3, 1) → (−3, −1). Or: calling it two reflections, one across each axis, counts. Partial: the transformation named with no test point is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","1.5, or 3/2. Check: −4 × 1.5 = −6 and 6 × 1.5 = 9. Or: −6 ÷ −4 and 9 ÷ 6 both give 1.5, and either ratio is enough. (8.G.A.3)","In a translation every point moves the same distance. T(2, 3) → T′(2, −3) is 6 units down, but V(2, 7) → V′(2, −7) is 14 units down. It is a reflection across the x-axis: each y-coordinate changes sign and each x-coordinate stays the same. Partial: showing the distances differ with no transformation named is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","Jo turned the point clockwise. Counterclockwise uses (x, y) → (−y, x), so the correct point is (5, 3). Partial: the right point, (5, 3), with no account of Jo's mistake is approaching. (8.G.A.3)","(−3, 8). The translation is 3 units left and 4 units up: (x, y) → (x − 3, y + 4). Or: count the squares from (2, −3) to (−1, 1) and repeat the count from (0, 4). (8.G.A.3)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Maze Fact Check",short:"Fact Check",parts:[{std:["W.8.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:150,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Do numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"research skills (judging sources, choosing search terms, fixing plagiarism, and citing)",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>The two sources disagree about when the paths are cut. Which source would you trust? Give two reasons from the source information.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: corn. B: how corn maze paths are cut GPS. C: fun fall things to do.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student’s report says: <em>A design that once took a week to mark by hand can now be cut in one afternoon.</em> There are no quotation marks and no source. Fix it on your paper. Quote it correctly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, title, magazine, and date.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons, such as: it names the author, it comes from a farm magazine, it has a date, it gives exact details. Source B is an undated forum post by an unknown user who says “I heard” and “Pretty sure.” (W.8.8, credibility and accuracy)","B. It uses the key words from the research question, so the results will be about cutting maze paths. A is too broad and C is off topic. (W.8.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Elena Ruiz writes, “A design that once took a week to mark by hand can now be cut in one afternoon.” Paraphrase: According to Elena Ruiz, GPS lets a farm cut in a few hours a maze that used to take days to mark. The student must name the source. (W.8.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","Has all four parts. Example: Ruiz, Elena. “Mapping a Maze with GPS.” Farm Technology Review, September 2025. Any clear, consistent order is fine. (W.8.8, citation)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Patch Press",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"informative",label:"Explain",icon:"📰",hint:"How a pumpkin gets from the vine to the store"},std:["W.8.2"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:"Write on your paper for 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an informative writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to explain how a pumpkin gets from the vine to a store without rotting. Use the fact box. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: from field to store",["A pumpkin is ready when its rind is hard and its vine starts to dry.","Workers cut the stem with clippers and leave a few inches on the pumpkin.","A pumpkin with a broken stem rots faster.","Pumpkins sit in the sun for about 10 days. This is called curing.","Curing hardens the rind and heals small cuts.","Cured pumpkins are packed into big cardboard bins. A forklift loads the bins onto trucks."])}${checklist(["Introduce the topic","Put the steps in an order that makes sense","Explain the facts in your own sentences","Use linking words: first, after that, because, as a result","Use topic words: rind, stem, curing","End with a conclusion"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and lets the reader know what is coming (W.8.2)","Organizes the ideas in a logical order, such as ready, cut, cure, pack, ship (W.8.2)","Develops the topic with relevant facts, a definition, and details from the fact box, such as curing for about 10 days (W.8.2)","Uses transitions to connect ideas, such as first, after that, because, as a result (W.8.2)","Uses precise language and topic words, such as rind, stem, curing, and keeps a formal style (W.8.2)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from the explanation (W.8.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"narrative",label:"Story",icon:"📖",hint:"Closing time in the corn maze"},std:["W.8.3"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:"Write on your paper for 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You work at the corn maze. At closing time, you walk the paths to make sure everyone is out. Near the center, you hear a small dog barking. Write the story of what happens. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Set the scene: where you are, and who is telling the story","Tell what happens in order","Include at least one line of dialogue","Use details the reader can see, hear, or feel","Use time words: at first, a moment later, by the time, finally","End by showing what the narrator learned or felt"])}`,key:["Sets up the situation and point of view and introduces the narrator or characters (W.8.3)","Events unfold in an order that is natural and logical (W.8.3)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, description, or reflection (W.8.3)","Uses transition words to show sequence and shifts in time or place (W.8.3)","Uses precise words, descriptive details, and sensory language (W.8.3)","Ends with a conclusion that follows from and reflects on the events (W.8.3)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"argument",label:"Argue",icon:"⚖️",hint:"Corn maze or weigh-off: which plan is better?"},std:["W.8.1"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:"Write on your paper for 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an argument writing piece with evidence from a chart",html:()=>`<p class="big">Goldfield Farm can add one new event next fall. Which plan is better? Write an argument. Use facts from the chart. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${bigTable(["","Plan A: Corn maze","Plan B: Giant pumpkin weigh-off"],[["Cost to set up","about $3,000","about $1,500"],["Ticket money expected","about $7,200 over 6 weekends","about $4,500 in 1 day"],["Work","workers needed every weekend","one very busy day"],["Biggest risk","a wet fall means muddy paths","rain on the one day"]])}${checklist(["State your claim","Give two reasons with facts from the chart","Name the other plan’s best point, and tell why your plan is still better","Use linking words: because, however, as a result","End with a conclusion"])}`,key:["Introduces a clear claim: Plan A or Plan B. Either plan can earn full credit. (W.8.1)","Names the other side’s claim or best point and shows how the student’s claim is different or stronger (W.8.1)","Supports the claim with logical reasons and relevant evidence from the chart. For reference: the maze brings in about $7,200 − $3,000 = $4,200 after costs, and the weigh-off about $4,500 − $1,500 = $3,000. (W.8.1)","Uses words and phrases that connect the claim, reasons, evidence, and the other side, such as because, however, as a result (W.8.1)","Keeps a formal style (W.8.1)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from and supports the argument (W.8.1)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Weigh the Claims",short:"Claims",parts:[{title:"Read the Op-Ed",std:["RI.8.8"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Read the opinion piece to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"delineating an argument and judging whether the evidence is relevant and sufficient",html:()=>F.passage(OPED,OPED_TITLE,true),qs:["What is the writer's claim, and what reasons back it?","One sentence is irrelevant to the claim. Which one, and why does it not help?","Is the evidence enough to change the rule? Say what is missing."],key:["Claim: the county should drop the display-only rule for a soft spot. Reasons: a coin-sized soft spot does not change the weight, which is what the contest measures; honest growers who report a bruise lose a ribbon; the rot risk can be handled by weighing those pumpkins last. (RI.8.8, delineate the argument)","The sentence about the rule being eleven years old and the fair being bigger than ever. The size of the fair says nothing about whether the rule is fair, and if anything it argues for keeping the rule. (RI.8.8, irrelevant evidence)","Not yet. The only case given is one grower, with no numbers on how often it happens, no word from the judges, and no evidence about whether a soft spot really does spread rot at the fairgrounds. (RI.8.8, sufficient evidence)"]},{title:"Where They Conflict",std:["RI.8.9"],n:2,dur:45,dir:"Read the two sources. Then answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing two texts that give conflicting information on the same topic",html:()=>SOURCES(),qs:["On what point of fact do these two sources disagree? Quote the words from each one.","Which account is more likely to be accurate, and what in the sources makes you say so?"],key:["When and how the paths get cut. Source A: Most large corn mazes are cut early, when the corn is about a foot tall, by a tractor with a GPS receiver. Source B: they wait until the corn is fully grown and then cut the paths with a helicopter. (RI.8.9, identify where the texts disagree)","Source A. It names an author, a magazine, and a date, and it gives specific detail. Source B is an undated forum post by an unknown user whose own words, I heard and Pretty sure, show the writer is not certain. (RI.8.9, matters of fact and interpretation)"]}]},{id:15,title:"One Scale or Two",short:"Pitch",parts:[{std:["SL.8.4"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:50,dir:"Plan for 20 seconds. Then talk for about 45 seconds. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a short spoken claim backed by sound reasoning and relevant evidence",html:()=>`<p class="big">The fair board asks you: should the weigh-off rent a second scale next year? Make your case.</p>${factBox("What you know",["90 pumpkins were entered this year.","There is one scale.","Each weighing takes about 4 minutes, start to finish.","The weigh-off is scheduled for 4 hours.","Renting a second scale costs $400 for the day."])}`,key:["States a clear claim: rent a second scale, or do not. Either claim can earn full credit. (SL.8.4)","The reasoning is sound and the numbers are used correctly. For reference: 90 x 4 minutes is 360 minutes, which is 6 hours, and the event is scheduled for 4. With two scales it is about 3 hours. (SL.8.4)","Uses relevant evidence rather than every fact in the box, and leaves out or answers the $400 cost rather than ignoring it. (SL.8.4)","Looks at the camera, speaks loudly enough, and pronounces words clearly. (SL.8.4)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Two Stands, Two Plans",short:"Lines",parts:[{title:"Price Per Pumpkin",std:["8.EE.B.5"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Stand A is a table. Stand B is a graph, with pumpkins across and dollars up. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing two proportional relationships shown in different ways and reading slope as a unit rate",fig:()=>F.row([F.table(["Pumpkins","Cost at Stand A"],[["2","$12"],["5","$30"],["8","$48"]]),"Stand A"],[F.coordGrid({min:0,max:20,size:300,points:[[4,20,"(4, 20)"]],lines:[[[0,0],[4,20]]],xLabel:"",yLabel:"dollars"}),"Stand B"]),qs:["What does Stand A charge for one pumpkin? Where do you see that in the table?","What is the slope of Stand B's line, and what does it mean here?","Which stand charges more per pumpkin? How would the two graphs look side by side?"],key:["$6. Every row divides to the same unit rate: 12/2 = 6, 30/5 = 6, 48/8 = 6. Or: read the slope off a graph of the table instead. Partial: $6 with no place in the table pointed to is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)","5. The line passes through (0, 0) and (4, 20), so the slope is 20/4 = 5, which is $5 per pumpkin. Or: read any one point on the line and divide, since the line goes through the origin. Partial: 5 with no account of what it means is approaching. (8.EE.B.5, unit rate as slope)","Stand A, by $1 per pumpkin. Stand A's line would be steeper, since a larger unit rate gives a steeper slope. Both lines start at (0, 0). Partial: the right stand with nothing said about the two graphs is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)"]},{title:"When Are They Equal",std:["8.EE.C.8"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"writing and solving a system of two linear equations in context",html:()=>`<p class="big">The corn maze sells two plans. Pay as you go costs $6 a visit. The season pass costs $10 once, plus $4 a visit.</p>`,qs:["Write a system of two equations for the total cost y after x visits.","Solve the system. What does the solution mean at the maze?","Which plan costs less for 8 visits? Show the numbers."],key:["y = 6x and y = 4x + 10. Partial: one of the two equations right is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","x = 5 and y = 30. At 5 visits the two plans cost the same, $30. Setting 6x = 4x + 10 gives 2x = 10. On a graph it is the point where the two lines cross. Or: substitution, elimination, a table or a graph all count. Partial: x = 5 with no cost given is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)","The season pass. 8 visits pay as you go is 6 x 8 = $48. The season pass is 4 x 8 + 10 = $42, which is $6 less. Partial: naming the pass with no numbers is approaching. (8.EE.C.8)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Vines and Functions",short:"Functions",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the table. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding whether a relation is a function, and telling linear rules from nonlinear ones",fig:()=>F.table(["Wagon number","Load in pounds"],[["1","120"],["2","240"],["3","360"],["2","300"]],{caption:"Loads logged at the barn"}),qs:["Is this table a function from wagon number to load? Explain using the rule for a function.","The log is fixed so wagon 2 appears only once, with 240. Write the rule as an equation and say whether it is linear.","Is A = s squared, the area of a square plot with side s, a linear function? How can you tell?"],key:["No. Wagon 2 is paired with two different loads, 240 and 300, and a function gives exactly one output for each input. Partial: no with no account of the repeated input is approaching. (8.F.A.1)","y = 120x. It is linear: it has the form y = mx + b with m = 120 and b = 0, and each wagon adds the same 120 pounds. Partial: the equation right with no answer about linearity is approaching. (8.F.A.3)","No, it is nonlinear. The outputs for s = 1, 2, 3 are 1, 4, 9, so the jumps are 3 and then 5 instead of the same amount every time. A linear function has a constant rate of change. Or: sketch it and show the graph curves. (8.F.A.3)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Across the Field",short:"Field Math",parts:[{title:"The Shortcut",std:["8.G.B.7"],n:2,dur:45,dir:"Draw a sketch on your paper if it helps. Tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"applying the Pythagorean theorem to find a distance in a real situation",qs:["A maze path runs 40 meters east, then turns and runs 30 meters north. A straight mowed shortcut joins the start to the end. How long is the shortcut?","A 13-foot brace runs from the top of a 12-foot pole down to the ground. How far from the base of the pole does the brace meet the ground?"],key:["50 meters. 40 squared is 1,600 and 30 squared is 900. The sum is 2,500, and the square root of 2,500 is 50. Or: recognize the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 10. Partial: 2,500 found with no square root taken is approaching. (8.G.B.7)","5 feet. Here the 13-foot brace is the hypotenuse: 13 squared minus 12 squared is 169 - 144 = 25, and the square root of 25 is 5. Or: recognize the 5, 12, 13 triple. Partial: adding instead of subtracting, giving √313, does not count. (8.G.B.7)"]},{title:"Weeks and Weight",std:["8.SP.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the scatter plot. Each dot is one pumpkin. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a scatter plot: association, linear or nonlinear, and outliers",fig:()=>F.row(F.coordGrid({min:0,max:24,size:340,xLabel:"weeks",yLabel:"tens of lb",points:[[2,1,""],[4,3,""],[6,6,""],[8,10,""],[10,15,""],[12,21,""],[7,2,""]]})),qs:["The across axis is weeks since the vine flowered. The up axis is weight in tens of pounds, so 10 means 100 pounds. Describe the association between the two.","Is the pattern linear or nonlinear? Use the numbers to say why.","One dot sits at (7, 2). What do we call a point like that, and what might explain it?"],key:["A positive association. As the number of weeks goes up, the weight goes up too. Partial: positive named with no account of what goes up with what is approaching. (8.SP.A.1)","Nonlinear. Following the main group, the weights go 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, so the jumps grow: 2, then 3, 4, 5, 6. A linear pattern would rise by the same amount every two weeks. Or: notice the dots curve away from any straight line drawn through them. Partial: nonlinear with no numbers is approaching. (8.SP.A.1)","An outlier. It sits far below the pattern: at 7 weeks that pumpkin is only 20 pounds, when the trend says about 80. A damaged vine, a late start, or a measuring mistake could explain it. Partial: naming it an outlier with no possible reason is approaching. (8.SP.A.1)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Before the Framing",short:"Framing",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the weaknesses of the Articles of Confederation and why they led to a new constitution",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Articles of Confederation governed the United States from 1781 to 1789. The chart lists what the national government could and could not do under them.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Could</th><th>Could not</th></tr>
     <tr><td>declare war and make treaties</td><td>levy any tax</td></tr>
     <tr><td>borrow money</td><td>raise an army on its own</td></tr>
     <tr><td>run a post office</td><td>regulate trade between the states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>admit new states</td><td>enforce its own requests on a state</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">There was no president and no national court. Nine of thirteen states had to agree to pass anything, and all thirteen to amend it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["The government could borrow money but could not tax. Explain why that combination could not last.","States put tariffs on goods from other states. What would that do to a merchant in New Jersey, and which later constitutional power fixes it?","In 1786 and 1787 indebted farmers in western Massachusetts shut down courts to stop foreclosures, and the national government had no force to send. How did that episode change the argument about the Articles?"],key:["A borrower with no revenue cannot repay, so lenders stop lending and the existing debt goes unpaid. The national government had to ask the states for money and they often sent little or none, which meant soldiers went unpaid, the war debt sat there and the country's credit collapsed. A government that cannot raise money cannot do anything else it is authorized to do. Partial: naming the debt with nothing said about the states sending little or nothing is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","It made goods more expensive the moment they crossed a state line, so a merchant faced a different set of duties in every market and interstate business became slow and costly. The Constitution fixes it by giving Congress alone the power to regulate commerce among the states and barring states from taxing imports and exports. Partial: naming the problem with no fix from the Constitution named is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","It turned a complaint about inefficiency into an argument about survival. Supporters of a stronger national government could now say that the existing one could not keep order or protect property anywhere, and that the states could not be relied on to do it either. Within months a convention was called, and it went well beyond revising the Articles. Partial: describing the rising with nothing said about what it changed in the argument is approaching. (HSS-8.1)"]},{std:["HSS-8.2"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading Article I Section 8 and distinguishing enumerated from implied powers in concrete cases",html:()=>`<p class="big">Some of the powers Article I gives Congress, in plain words.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Congress may</th></tr>
     <tr><td>lay and collect taxes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>borrow money</td></tr>
     <tr><td>regulate commerce with foreign nations and among the states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>coin money and set its value</td></tr>
     <tr><td>establish post offices and post roads</td></tr>
     <tr><td>raise and support armies</td></tr>
     <tr><td>make all laws necessary and proper for carrying the above into execution</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("corn",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Congress builds an interstate highway system. Which row or rows let it, and is the power enumerated or implied?","Congress sets a minimum wage for workers at a factory that ships its goods to other states. Which row is the argument built on, and what is the counterargument?","In your own words, what is the difference between an enumerated power and an implied power?"],key:["Mainly the commerce power, with support from post roads and from raising and supporting armies. Highways are not named, so the power is implied: Congress reaches it through the necessary and proper clause as a means of carrying out enumerated powers it already has. Accept the taxing and spending power as an additional route. Partial: naming the power with no account of it being implied rather than listed is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","The commerce power, on the argument that wages are part of the conditions under which goods move between states, so Congress may regulate them. The counterargument is that making a thing is not the same as trading it, and that if producing goods counts as commerce there is almost nothing left that Congress could not regulate, which would erase the limits a list of powers is for. Partial: one of the argument and the counterargument given is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","An enumerated power is written out in the Constitution itself, so the question is only what the words mean. An implied power is not written out: it is claimed as a reasonable means of carrying out a power that is. That is why arguments about implied powers are arguments about how far necessary and proper stretches. Partial: one of the two kinds of power defined is approaching. (HSS-8.2)"]},{std:["HSS-8.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the rights and responsibilities of citizenship and how a person becomes a citizen",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two paths to United States citizenship.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>By birth</th><th>By naturalization</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Who</td><td>anyone born in the country, and children born abroad to citizen parents</td><td>a lawful permanent resident who applies</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Requirements</td><td>none</td><td>usually five years of residence, good moral character, a test on English and on United States history and government, and an oath</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Rights gained</td><td>all</td><td>all except eligibility for the presidency</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Citizenship by birth is written into the Fourteenth Amendment. Why would the framers of that amendment have wanted a rule that depends on nothing but where you were born?","Name two rights a citizen has that a lawful resident who is not a citizen does not, and two responsibilities both share.","The naturalization test covers United States history and government. What is the reasoning behind testing that in particular?"],key:["Because they had just seen citizenship denied to an entire group by a court decision. A rule based on the plain fact of birth cannot be argued away by a legislature or a judge deciding that some people do not qualify, so it put the citizenship of freedpeople and their children beyond the reach of ordinary politics. Partial: naming birthright citizenship with nothing said about what it put beyond reach is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Rights: voting in federal elections, serving on a federal jury, holding most elected offices, and a United States passport. Accept any two. Responsibilities both share: obeying the law, paying taxes, and registering for selective service where it applies. Partial: one of the rights and the shared responsibilities answered is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","The reasoning is that participation requires knowing how the system works: a person who does not know what the branches do, how a law is made or what rights the Constitution protects cannot use any of it well, nor recognize when it is being misused. Whether a test is the right way to ensure that is a fair thing to argue about. Partial: a yes or no with no reasoning about what participation requires is approaching. (HSS-8.3)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Growing Outward",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing public education and an American language as aspirations of the new nation",html:()=>`<p class="big">Noah Webster thought a new nation needed its own words. He published a spelling book in 1783 that taught American spellings rather than British ones, and over the next century it sold tens of millions of copies. He then spent twenty years on an American dictionary. Meanwhile states began paying for common schools open to all children, and by the 1850s Massachusetts and several other states required towns to run them. Horace Mann, who ran the Massachusetts schools, argued that a republic could not survive on an ignorant electorate.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why would spelling be something a new country cared about?","State Mann's argument for making schools public and free, in your own words.","Common schools were a real achievement and they were not open to everyone. Name one group left out and how."],key:["Because a country that had just separated politically from Britain was still reading British books, using British spellings and learning from British schoolmasters, and a distinct written language was a visible mark of being a separate people. Webster also argued a common American spelling would hold a scattered country together. Partial: naming the spellings with nothing said about being a separate people is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","That in a republic ordinary voters decide, so the nation's safety depends on their judgment, and judgment requires knowledge. If only the children of the wealthy are educated, then either the republic is run by the ignorant or it stops being a republic. Public money for schools was therefore not charity but self defense. Partial: saying education is good with no link to voters deciding is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Any accurate answer: in most Southern states it was illegal to teach enslaved children to read at all; Black children in the North were often barred from common schools or sent to separate and poorer ones; girls were admitted in many places but steered away from advanced study; and children whose labor their families needed could not attend regularly whatever the law said. Partial: naming one group left out with no reason is approaching. (HSS-8.4)"]},{std:["HSS-8.5"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Lewis and Clark expedition as an instrument of foreign and territorial policy",html:()=>`<p class="big">In 1804 Jefferson sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark up the Missouri River with about forty men. Their written orders told them to find a water route to the Pacific, to record the plants, animals, soil and weather, to map what they crossed, and to tell every Native nation they met that the United States was now the governing power and wanted its trade. They carried medals and flags to give out. Sacagawea, a Shoshone woman, traveled with them and interpreted; her presence and her infant son also signaled to the nations they met that the party was not a war party. They reached the Pacific in 1805 and returned in 1806.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("canoe",74)}${ico("map",74)}${ico("compass",74)}</div>`,qs:["The orders mix science, mapping and diplomacy. Why would one expedition be given all three jobs?","There was no water route across the continent. Was the expedition therefore a failure? Defend your answer with the orders.","The party told Native nations that the United States was now the governing power. From the point of view of those nations, what had actually changed and what had not?"],key:["Because all three served the same purpose: to find out what the country had just bought and to establish a claim to it. Maps and specimens told the government what the land was worth, and announcing authority and opening trade were how a claim on paper was turned into a real relationship on the ground before Britain or Spain got there first. Partial: one of the three purposes named is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","No. Finding there was no easy water route was itself valuable information, and the expedition fulfilled most of its written orders: it mapped the route, recorded well over a hundred new plants and animals, made contact with dozens of nations, and reached the Pacific and came back. Settling a question in the negative is still settling it. Partial: calling it a failure because no water route was found is approaching, since settling the question was itself the result. (HSS-8.5)","On paper the claim to govern had passed from France to the United States, but no nation they met had agreed to any of it, and in practice these nations still controlled their own territory, trade and relations for decades. What had changed was that a new and growing power was now asserting a claim and would keep pressing it, and the expedition's maps and reports made later American expansion possible. Partial: one of the paper claim and the practical reality named is approaching. (HSS-8.5)"]},{std:["HSS-8.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing immigration into Northeastern cities in the 1840s and 1850s and the reaction to it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Arrivals into the United States, most of them landing at Northeastern ports and staying in Northeastern cities.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Decade</th><th>Immigrants arriving</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1830s</td><td>about 600,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1840s</td><td>about 1,700,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850s</td><td>about 2,600,000</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Most came from Ireland, where the potato crop failed repeatedly after 1845, and from the German states after the failed revolutions of 1848. By 1855 more than half the residents of New York City had been born abroad.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("bread",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name one push factor and one pull factor in this movement.","Why would newly arrived families end up in crowded city neighborhoods rather than on western farms?","A political movement formed in the 1850s on the platform of restricting immigration and barring immigrants from office. What were its supporters afraid of, and which of those fears would you say the evidence supports?"],key:["Push: crop failure and hunger in Ireland, and political repression after the 1848 revolutions in the German states. Pull: wages in canal, railroad and factory work, cheap western land, and relatives already settled who could take a family in. A strong answer names one of each rather than two of the same kind. Partial: two reasons of the same kind, both push or both pull, is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Money. A farm took cash for land, tools, seed, stock and the journey, and most arrivals landed with almost nothing, so they took the work at hand in the port city they arrived in. Many German families who did arrive with some savings went on to farm in Ohio, Wisconsin and Missouri, which shows the difference capital made. Partial: naming the cities with nothing said about the cost of a farm is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Supporters feared competition for jobs and lower wages, the cost of poor relief, loss of political control in cities where immigrant voters were numerous, and the arrival of people whose customs they considered foreign. On the evidence, the labor competition was real in specific trades, while the claims about disloyalty and incapacity were not: these arrivals built the canals, worked the mills and within a generation ran the cities. Partial: listing the fears with no judgment on which held up against the evidence is approaching. (HSS-8.6)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Three Ways of Living",short:"Sections",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.7"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing what slavery was in law and in daily life in the South",html:()=>`<p class="big">Slavery in the Southern states was a system set out in law. A person held in slavery was legally the property of another person: they could be bought, sold, mortgaged, given away and inherited. They received no wages. They could not leave, choose an employer, or travel without a written pass. Their marriages had no legal standing and their children belonged to their owner. Most Southern states made it a crime to teach them to read. They could not testify in court against a white person, so an injury done to them by one was very hard to prove. Work was from before dawn to after dark, six days a week, under an overseer.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("corn",74)}${ico("chest",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["Pick the restriction you think was most important to holding the whole system together, and defend your choice.","Teaching a person to read was made a crime. What were those laws protecting?","Being unable to testify against a white person was a rule about courts. Explain its effect outside the courtroom."],key:["Any defended choice. Strong answers: being property, because every other restriction follows from it; the pass system, because a system of forced labor depends on people not being able to leave; or the ban on literacy, because reading is how a person learns, organizes and makes a case for themselves. The reasoning is what is being rated, not which one is picked. Partial: a choice with no reasoning is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","They were protecting the system from information and from organization. A person who can read can read a map, a pass, a contract, an abolitionist newspaper or the Declaration of Independence, and can write to someone far away. They were also protecting the claim that enslaved people were incapable of learning, which is the claim the whole justification rested on. Partial: naming the fear of organizing with nothing said about the claim the justification rested on is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","It meant that a white person could do almost anything to an enslaved person with little risk, because the only witness who could usually be produced was barred from speaking. That turned a rule of evidence into near immunity, and it is why laws that formally limited an owner's conduct had so little practical force. Partial: naming the rule with nothing said about it amounting to near immunity is approaching. (HSS-8.7)"]},{std:["HSS-8.8"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Texas sequence from Mexican province to American state",html:()=>`<p class="big">How Texas changed hands.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1821</td><td>Mexico wins independence from Spain and invites American settlers into Texas on condition they become Mexican citizens</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1829</td><td>Mexico abolishes slavery; American settlers in Texas largely ignore the law</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1830</td><td>Mexico bans further American immigration; settlers keep coming</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1836</td><td>Texas declares independence and wins it, and its constitution legalizes slavery</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1845</td><td>The United States annexes Texas as a slave state</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("horse",74)}${ico("flag",74)}</div>`,qs:["Mexico invited Americans in and then tried to stop them. What had gone wrong from Mexico's point of view?","Slavery appears twice on the chart. Explain how it runs through this sequence.","Annexation sat in Congress for nine years before it passed. Why would admitting Texas be controversial in Washington?"],key:["Mexico wanted settlers to populate a thinly held northern province and become Mexican citizens who would defend it. Instead the settlers kept their own language, ignored Mexican law including the abolition of slavery, outnumbered the Mexican residents within a decade, and then left. The policy produced a population loyal to another country. Partial: naming the invitation with nothing said about what the policy produced is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","Mexico outlawed it and the American settlers kept it anyway, which was one of their grievances against Mexican authority. Independent Texas then wrote it into its own constitution, and annexation added a large slave state to the Union. The dispute over slavery is not a side issue in this sequence, it runs the length of it. Partial: naming one stage with no thread run through the sequence is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","Because Texas was enormous and would enter as a slave state, which shifted the balance in the Senate and in the electoral college, and because annexing territory Mexico still claimed meant a likely war. Northern members blocked it on both grounds until the 1844 election was read as a mandate and it passed by joint resolution. Partial: one of the slavery balance and the likely war named is approaching. (HSS-8.8)"]},{std:["HSS-8.9"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Underground Railroad and the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850",html:()=>`<p class="big">People escaping slavery moved north along routes kept by a loose network of helpers. A guide was called a conductor and a safe house a station. Harriet Tubman escaped from Maryland in 1849 and then went back repeatedly, bringing out some seventy people over about a decade without losing one. The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 made this far harder: it required federal marshals and ordinary citizens anywhere in the country to assist in capture, denied the accused a jury trial or the right to testify, and paid the deciding commissioner more for a ruling in favor of the claimant than against.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("star",74)}${ico("pine",74)}</div>`,qs:["The 1850 law reached into free states. Why would that change how Northerners who had taken no side thought about slavery?","Name two features of the law that made a fair hearing nearly impossible.","Tubman had reached freedom and went back more than a dozen times. What does the network's structure tell you about why that was possible at all?"],key:["Because it made them participants. A Northerner could now be required to help seize a neighbor, and could be fined or jailed for refusing, so slavery stopped being a distant Southern institution and became a demand on their own conduct. Resistance and public anger in the North rose sharply after 1850 for exactly that reason. Partial: saying it was unpopular with no account of what it required of Northerners is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Any two: no jury trial, the accused could not testify in their own defense, the commissioner was paid more for returning a person than for releasing one, a claimant's affidavit was treated as sufficient proof, and free Black people seized by mistake or by fraud had almost no way to establish their status. Partial: one feature named is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","It worked because it was decentralized and local: no one person knew the whole network, helpers knew only the next station, and routes shifted. That meant a betrayal in one place did not bring down the rest, and someone who knew the ground could travel it repeatedly. It also depended on many ordinary people taking a real legal risk. Partial: describing the network with nothing said about why decentralizing protected it is approaching. (HSS-8.9)"]}]},{id:22,title:"The War and What Followed",short:"War and After",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.10"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"ordering the key events of the Civil War and explaining why each mattered",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four years, in six entries.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th></tr>
     <tr><td>April 1861</td><td>Confederate guns fire on Fort Sumter in Charleston harbor</td></tr>
     <tr><td>September 1862</td><td>Antietam: the Confederate advance into Maryland is turned back</td></tr>
     <tr><td>January 1863</td><td>The Emancipation Proclamation takes effect</td></tr>
     <tr><td>July 1863</td><td>Gettysburg and Vicksburg, on consecutive days</td></tr>
     <tr><td>late 1864</td><td>Union armies cut through Georgia, destroying railroads and supplies</td></tr>
     <tr><td>April 1865</td><td>Lee surrenders at Appomattox Court House</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("map",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["Antietam was not a clear victory, and Lincoln used it anyway. For what, and why did he need a battle first?","Vicksburg gave the Union control of the whole Mississippi. Why was that worth as much as a battlefield victory?","Pick the entry you would call the turning point and defend it against one other choice."],key:["To issue the Emancipation Proclamation. He had drafted it earlier but held it back, because announcing emancipation after a string of defeats would have read as a desperate measure rather than a decision made from strength. Turning back the invasion gave him the moment. Partial: naming the Proclamation with nothing said about why he needed a victory first is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Because it split the Confederacy in two and cut the eastern states off from Texas cattle and grain, and it reopened the river to Northern and Western shipping. Controlling supply and transport decides what armies can do, so a river is a strategic object in its own right. Partial: naming the river with nothing said about supply or transport is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Any defended choice with a real comparison. Antietam, because it enabled emancipation and ended hope of British recognition. July 1863, because the Confederacy lost its last offensive capacity in the east and the Mississippi on the same weekend. The Proclamation, because it changed what a Union victory would mean and brought Black soldiers into the army. The defense and the comparison are what is rated. Partial: a choice defended with no comparison to another is approaching. (HSS-8.10)"]},{std:["HSS-8.11"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Freedmen's Bureau, what it accomplished and why it ended",html:()=>`<p class="big">Congress created the Bureau of Refugees, Freedmen and Abandoned Lands in 1865. It was meant to last one year. It handed out food and medical care to both Black and white southerners left destitute by the war, helped set up and supply more than four thousand schools, witnessed labor contracts between freedpeople and landowners so that wages had to be written down, and ran courts where freedpeople could bring complaints. It was always understaffed, with a few hundred agents for an entire region, and Congress let it expire in 1872.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("bread",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Witnessing contracts sounds like paperwork. Why did it matter so much in 1866?","A few hundred agents covered eleven states. What does that tell you about how much the Bureau could actually enforce?","The Bureau helped white southerners as well. Why would Congress write it that way?"],key:["Because freedpeople were negotiating with former owners who held the land, the tools and the local courts, and a verbal promise of wages was worth nothing. A written contract witnessed by a federal agent gave a worker something to point to, and a place to complain to when it was broken. It was the one piece of leverage most freedpeople had. Partial: naming the contract with nothing said about who held the land and the courts is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","That it depended on persuasion and on the threat of federal attention rather than on real enforcement. An agent could not be present at thousands of farms, local officials often ignored him, and a complaint required traveling to reach him. It explains how the Bureau achieved a great deal in schooling and still could not protect people's wages or safety reliably. Partial: naming the shortage of agents with nothing said about what the Bureau could and could not achieve is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Partly because the war had ruined much of the South and left poor white families hungry too, so relief by need rather than by race was both humane and practical. It also blunted the charge that the Bureau existed only to favor one group, and it gave northern members of Congress a reason to vote for it. Partial: one of the humane and the political reasons given is approaching. (HSS-8.11)"]},{std:["HSS-8.12"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing how factory work changed and what that meant for workers",html:()=>`<p class="big">What work looked like before and after the shift to large factories.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>A craft shop, 1840</th><th>A large factory, 1890</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Who decided the pace</td><td>the craftsman</td><td>the machine and the foreman</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Skill needed</td><td>years of training in the whole trade</td><td>weeks, for one repeated step</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Typical week</td><td>about 60 hours</td><td>about 60 hours, often six ten hour days</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Chance of advancing</td><td>journeyman, then owning a shop</td><td>very limited</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">In 1900 about 1,750,000 children aged 10 to 15 worked for wages in mills, mines and canneries.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("box",74)}${ico("clock",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Breaking a trade into single repeated steps was efficient for the owner. What did it cost the worker?","Why would factory owners hire children, and why would a family send one?","A craftsman who disliked his terms could set up his own shop. What options replaced that one after 1890, and why did they take collective action?"],key:["Bargaining power and standing. A worker trained for one step could be replaced in a week by anyone off the street, so he could not threaten to leave, and the path from apprentice to shop owner was gone. Wages and conditions were set by the employer rather than negotiated between skilled equals, and the work itself became monotonous. Partial: naming the dull work with nothing said about being replaceable is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Owners hired children because they were paid a fraction of an adult wage, were small enough for some machine work, and were unlikely to organize. Families sent them because adult wages often did not cover rent and food, so a child's two or three dollars a week was the difference. That is why bans on child labor had to come with wage and schooling laws to work. Partial: one of the owners' and the families' reasons given is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","What replaced it was moving to another employer on the same terms, or acting together: unions, strikes, boycotts and demands for laws on hours and safety. They acted collectively because individually each worker was replaceable, while all of them at once were not. That is the logic behind the Knights of Labor and the American Federation of Labor. Partial: naming the unions with nothing said about why they acted together is approaching. (HSS-8.12)"]}]},{id:23,title:"What Makes a Rind Hard",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a change in a gene through the protein it codes for to the trait that results",html:()=>`<p class="big">The pumpkins in this patch carry five versions of one gene. The gene codes for an enzyme that cross links the cell walls of the rind, which is what makes a rind hard.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gene version</th><th>Protein the gene codes for</th><th>Rind</th></tr>
     <tr><td>H1, unchanged</td><td>full length enzyme, normal amount</td><td>hard, keeps until spring</td></tr>
     <tr><td>H2, one base changed beside the active site</td><td>enzyme folds but works at a quarter the normal rate</td><td>softer, keeps about two months</td></tr>
     <tr><td>H3, a stop signal created early in the gene</td><td>only a third of the protein is built and it has no activity</td><td>thin, splits open on the vine</td></tr>
     <tr><td>H4, change in the switch stretch that turns the gene on</td><td>the same enzyme, twice as much of it</td><td>very hard, blunts a knife</td></tr>
     <tr><td>H5, change in a nearby stretch that is never read</td><td>the same enzyme, the same amount</td><td>hard, keeps until spring</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}</div>`,qs:["H4 and H5 both leave the enzyme's amino acids untouched, and only one of them changes the rind. Explain the difference.","Put H1, H2 and H3 in order of how much enzyme activity each has, and say how the rind follows that order.","A worker says every mutation is a defect. Use the chart to answer, naming a version that helps, one that hurts and one that does neither."],key:["H4 falls in the stretch that controls when and how strongly the gene is switched on, so the cell builds twice as much of the same enzyme, more cross linking happens, and the rind ends up harder. H5 falls in a stretch that is never read into a protein and never used as a switch, so the cell builds the same enzyme in the same amount and nothing about the rind changes. A trait follows the protein and how much of it is present, so a change matters only if it reaches one of those. Partial: saying H4 changes how much enzyme is made with nothing said about H5 falling in a stretch that is never read is approaching. (LS3)","Most activity first: H4 has twice the normal activity, H1 has normal activity, H2 has a quarter of normal, H3 has none. The rind follows the same order, from blunting a knife, to hard and keeping until spring, to soft and keeping two months, to thin and splitting on the vine. More working enzyme means more cross linked wall and a harder rind, so the trait tracks the amount of working protein. Partial: the four versions put in the right order with nothing said about the rind following the amount of working enzyme is approaching. (LS3)","Helps, depending on what the grower wants: H4 gives a rind that keeps longest, which is an advantage for storage, though it is harder to cut. Hurts: H3 leaves no working enzyme, and a pumpkin that splits on the vine loses its seeds to rot. Neither: H5 is a real change in the DNA with no effect on the protein or the rind at all. So mutations are harmful, helpful or neutral depending on what they do to the protein, and which they are can depend on the conditions. Partial: two of the three cases named from the chart is approaching, and the neutral H5 is usually the one left out. (LS3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Jaws, Ears and the Well Pit",short:"Evolution",parts:[{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading a fossil sequence, vestigial structures and embryo stages as evidence of common ancestry",html:()=>`<p class="big">A well was dug at the edge of the patch and each layer was recorded. Layers pile up newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Rock</th><th>Animal found in it</th><th>Bones in the lower jaw</th><th>Bones in the middle ear</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, 0 to 3 m</td><td>field soil</td><td>mouse like animals alive today</td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2, 3 to 11 m</td><td>marl</td><td>small furred animal</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3, 11 to 24 m</td><td>mudstone</td><td>animal with both reptile and mammal features</td><td>4</td><td>2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, 24 to 40 m</td><td>sandstone</td><td>reptile like animal</td><td>6</td><td>1</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Structures that are present in a living animal but not used for their usual job.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Animal</th><th>Structure</th><th>Used for its usual job?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>python</td><td>two small hind limb bones inside the body wall</td><td>no, it has no legs</td></tr>
     <tr><td>kiwi</td><td>wing bones and a shoulder girdle</td><td>no, it cannot fly</td></tr>
     <tr><td>blind cave fish</td><td>eye sockets with eye tissue under the skin</td><td>no, it cannot see</td></tr>
     <tr><td>human</td><td>the muscles that swivel the outer ear</td><td>in most people, no</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mouse",74)}${ico("ear",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Read the layers from oldest to youngest and describe what happens to the jaw bones and the ear bones.","Use the second chart. Explain why a structure that is present and unused is evidence about ancestry, and say what would be hard to explain without that idea.","A python embryo starts two hind limb buds and then stops developing them. Say how that joins the two charts into one argument."],key:["Oldest first: layer 4 with 6 jaw bones and 1 ear bone, then layer 3 with 4 and 2, then layer 2 with 2 and 3, then layer 1 with 1 and 3. The jaw loses bones in step with the middle ear gaining them, which is the pattern expected if bones at the back of the jaw were taken over for hearing. The layers hold a sequence in which the same bones are reorganized, not a set of unrelated designs. Partial: the four layers in the right order with nothing said about the jaw losing bones in step with the ear gaining them is approaching. (LS4)","A structure that is present but useless is not something a design for the animal's present life would include. It makes sense if the animal inherited it from ancestors that did use it, and it has been reduced rather than removed. Without common ancestry there is nothing to explain why a legless snake carries hind limb bones, a flightless bird carries a flight shoulder, or a blind fish grows eye tissue it then covers over. Partial: saying the unused structures are leftovers, with nothing said about there being no other account of why they are there, is approaching. (LS4)","The embryo shows the limb instructions are still present and still switched on at the start, and only the later stages are cut short, which is exactly what a reduced inherited structure looks like from the inside. The well pit gives the sequence of change in time, the unused structures show leftovers in living animals, and the embryo shows the inherited instructions behind those leftovers. Fossil, anatomical and developmental evidence point the same way. Partial: saying the python still has the limb instructions with nothing said about the three kinds of evidence pointing the same way is approaching. (LS4)"]},{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both sets of numbers. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining natural selection from a trait distribution and distinguishing it from artificial selection",html:()=>`<p class="big">Wild gourds grow along the fence line. A hard frost now arrives in week 20 every autumn, and only plants that have set fruit before it leave any seed. One hundred and twenty plants were scored each generation for the week they first flowered.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Week of first flower</th><th>Generation 1</th><th>Generation 6</th><th>Generation 14</th></tr>
     <tr><td>week 8</td><td>9</td><td>24</td><td>51</td></tr>
     <tr><td>week 11</td><td>34</td><td>46</td><td>48</td></tr>
     <tr><td>week 14</td><td>48</td><td>38</td><td>19</td></tr>
     <tr><td>week 17</td><td>29</td><td>12</td><td>2</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The giant pumpkins on the show bench are a different story. Growers save seed only from their single heaviest fruit each year. The record rose from 180 kg in 1980 to 1,100 kg in 2024.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("snowflake",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why an early frost moves the distribution the way it does. Name what varies, what the frost does and what is inherited.","Work out the share flowering in week 8 in generation 1 and in generation 14, and say what the chart does not show about any single plant's flowering week.","Compare the gourds with the show bench pumpkins. Say what plays the part of the frost on the bench, and explain why the pumpkin record moved so far in so few generations."],key:["Flowering week already varies among the gourds and is inherited. A frost in week 20 ends the season, so plants that flowered in week 17 often have no ripe seed while plants that flowered in week 8 do, which makes early flowering plants far more likely to leave offspring. Those offspring inherit the early flowering gene versions, so the distribution shifts earlier every generation. Variation, a difference in the probability of reproducing, then inheritance. Partial: two of the three steps given is approaching, and the inheritance step is usually the one left out. (LS4)","Generation 1: 9 of 120, which is 7.5 percent. Generation 14: 51 of 120, which is 42.5 percent. Nothing in the chart says any plant moved its own flowering week forward during its life. What changed is the distribution across the population, which is the only thing natural selection changes. Partial: working out the 7.5 and 42.5 percent with nothing said about no individual plant having moved its own flowering week is approaching. (LS4)","On the bench the grower plays the part of the frost: the grower decides which single fruit supplies next year's seed, which is artificial selection. It moves faster because the selection is far more extreme and aimed at one trait only. The frost removes late plants but every surviving early plant still breeds, while the grower breeds from one fruit in a field, the very top of the distribution, so the average jumps instead of drifting. Partial: naming the grower as what plays the part of the frost with nothing said about breeding from one fruit being far more extreme selection is approaching. (LS4)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Tides at the Landing",short:"Universe",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining a tidal and lunar cycle and the part gravity plays in holding moons and planets",html:()=>`<p class="big">The river landing below the patch keeps a tide book. One month of high tides, with the moon noted.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Moon</th><th>Height of the high tide</th></tr>
     <tr><td>October 1</td><td>fully lit</td><td>2.9 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>October 8</td><td>half lit</td><td>2.1 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>October 15</td><td>not visible at all</td><td>3.0 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>October 22</td><td>half lit</td><td>2.0 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>October 29</td><td>fully lit</td><td>2.9 m</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("moon",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe how the tide height tracks the moon's phase, and explain the pattern using the positions of the sun, the moon and Earth.","A solar eclipse can only happen on one of these five dates. Say which one and why, and say why there was no eclipse that day.","Jupiter holds 95 known moons and Earth holds one. Name the force that keeps a moon with its planet and say what it is about Jupiter that lets it hold so many."],key:["The highest tides fall on the fully lit and the invisible moon, 2.9 m and 3.0 m, and the lowest on the two half lit dates, 2.1 m and 2.0 m. At full and new the sun, Earth and moon lie in a line, so the sun's pull and the moon's pull add together and the tidal bulge is largest. At the half lit phases the sun is at right angles to the moon as seen from Earth, so the two pulls work across each other and partly cancel, giving the smallest range. Partial: matching the biggest tides to the full and new moon with nothing said about the sun and moon pulls adding or working across each other is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","October 15, the one date the moon was not visible at all. A solar eclipse needs the moon between Earth and the sun, which is the same arrangement that leaves the moon's lit face turned away from us and makes it invisible. There was no eclipse because the moon's orbit is tilted about five degrees to Earth's path, so at most new moons the moon passes above or below the sun rather than across it. Partial: naming October 15 with nothing said about the tilted orbit being why there was no eclipse is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Gravity. Jupiter has about 318 times Earth's mass, and gravitational force grows with mass, so Jupiter's pull reaches far enough out and stays strong enough to hold many objects in orbit, including small ones captured from passing debris. Earth has far less mass, so the region where its pull dominates is much smaller, and the sun's pull takes over sooner. Partial: naming gravity with nothing said about Jupiter's far greater mass extending the region where its pull dominates is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]},{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating orbit distance to orbit time, building a scale model, and dating a fault from what it cuts",html:()=>`<p class="big">Five planets, with the distance to the sun given in astronomical units, where 1 AU is Earth's distance.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Planet</th><th>Distance from the sun in AU</th><th>One orbit, in Earth years</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Mercury</td><td>0.39</td><td>0.24</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Earth</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mars</td><td>1.52</td><td>1.88</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>5.20</td><td>11.86</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Saturn</td><td>9.58</td><td>29.40</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The well pit again, dated, with one extra feature.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Unit, top to bottom</th><th>Age of the rock</th></tr>
     <tr><td>field soil</td><td>6,000 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>marl</td><td>7 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>mudstone</td><td>90 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sandstone</td><td>210 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a crack that cuts through all four units, with everything on its west side dropped 2 m</td><td>not dated</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sun",74)}${ico("globe",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe how the orbit time changes with distance from the sun, and explain why gravity gives that result.","A model on the patch's 100 m lane puts Saturn at the far end. Work out where Earth and Jupiter go, and name one thing the model cannot show.","Say what you can establish about when the crack moved, and name the rule you used."],key:["The farther out a planet is, the longer its orbit takes, and the time stretches faster than the distance does: Saturn is about 9.6 times Earth's distance and takes about 29 years, not 9.6. Farther from the sun the pull is weaker, so a planet does not need to travel as fast to keep curving around, and it also has a far longer path to cover. A longer path at a lower speed gives a much longer year. Partial: saying the outer planets take longer with nothing said about the weaker pull allowing a lower speed over a longer path is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Saturn at 9.58 AU sits at 100 m, so 1 AU is 100 divided by 9.58, about 10.4 m. Earth goes at 10.4 m from the sun end and Jupiter at 5.20 times 10.4, about 54 m. On that scale Earth would be smaller than a grain of salt, so the model cannot show the planets' sizes at the same scale as their distances. It also cannot show that the orbits are not a straight line, and it cannot show that each planet moves at its own speed. Partial: placing Earth and Jupiter correctly with nothing named that the model cannot show is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","The crack has to be younger than everything it cuts, and it cuts the field soil, which is 6,000 years old, so the movement happened within the last 6,000 years. The rule is cross cutting relationships: a feature that cuts across rock units formed after those units were already there, which is why a fault can be placed in time even when the fault itself cannot be dated directly. Partial: dating the movement to within the last 6,000 years with the cross cutting rule not named is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"How Much Ground It Takes",short:"Human Impact",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"separating population growth from per person consumption in total demand on a resource",html:()=>`<p class="big">Cropland in this county, from the agricultural census. The county holds 62,000 hectares of land that can be farmed at all.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People</th><th>Hectares used per 1,000 people</th><th>Total hectares in crops</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1960</td><td>50,000</td><td>520</td><td>26,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1985</td><td>80,000</td><td>455</td><td>36,400</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2010</td><td>120,000</td><td>400</td><td>48,000</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2025</td><td>150,000</td><td>380</td><td>57,000</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}${ico("corn",74)}</div>`,qs:["Land used per 1,000 people fell by more than a quarter, and the total still rose by 31,000 hectares. Separate the two causes using the numbers.","Work out how many more people the county could add at the 2025 rate before it runs out of farmable land.","Name two effects on Earth's systems of pushing cropland up to the county's limit. Then say what a county that keeps growing has left to change once per person use is as low as it can go."],key:["People tripled, from 50,000 to 150,000, a factor of 3. Land per 1,000 people fell from 520 to 380 hectares, a factor of 0.73. Multiplied together, 3 times 0.73 is about 2.19, and 26,000 times 2.19 is about 57,000, which is the total. Population growth raised the total while rising efficiency pulled it back, and population won by a wide margin. Partial: naming both factors with only one of the two multipliers worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","62,000 hectares available less 57,000 already in crops leaves 5,000 hectares. At 380 hectares per 1,000 people, 5,000 hectares supports 5,000 divided by 380, which is about 13.2 thousand people, so roughly 13,000 more people. At the 1960 rate of 520 hectares per 1,000 it would be only about 9,600, so the gain in efficiency bought room for about 3,500 extra people. Partial: getting the 5,000 spare hectares with the 13,000 people not worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: natural habitat is converted, so the species that lived there lose their ground; soil is exposed and eroded, and its carbon and nutrients are drawn down; fertilizer and irrigation water move into streams and groundwater, changing both; and water drawn for irrigation lowers rivers and aquifers. Once per person use cannot fall further, the only factors left are the number of people, importing food and so using someone else's land, or raising the yield from each hectare already in crops. Partial: two effects named with nothing said about what a growing county has left to change once per person use is as low as it can go is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Two Carts on the Lane",short:"Forces",parts:[{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"applying the third law to a push between two objects and relating force and mass to motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two carts stand nose to nose on the flat lane with a squeezed spring between them. The spring is released.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cart</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed just after release</th></tr>
     <tr><td>cart of pumpkins</td><td>24 kg</td><td>1.5 m/s east</td></tr>
     <tr><td>cart of straw</td><td>36 kg</td><td>1.0 m/s west</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">One cart is then pushed along the lane with a measured force.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Push</th><th>Mass</th><th>Acceleration</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>48 N</td><td>24 kg</td><td>2 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>48 N</td><td>12 kg</td><td>4 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>96 N</td><td>24 kg</td><td>4 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>72 N</td><td>36 kg</td><td>2 m/s each second</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}</div>`,qs:["One spring pushed both carts. Explain with Newton's third law what each cart experienced, and compare the two forces.","Multiply mass by speed for each cart. Say what you find, and predict the straw cart's speed if the pumpkin cart had held 48 kg and still left at 1.5 m/s.","In the second chart, which two trials differ only in mass, and which two differ only in the push? Say what each pair shows, then test trial 4 against the rule."],key:["The spring pushed east on the pumpkin cart and west on the straw cart with forces equal in size and opposite in direction, at the same moment and for the same length of time. Neither cart is special; whatever the spring did to one, the other had done to it. Both are free to roll, so both move. Partial: saying both carts were pushed, with nothing said about the two forces being equal in size and opposite in direction, is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Pumpkin cart: 24 times 1.5 is 36. Straw cart: 36 times 1.0 is also 36. The two quantities of motion are equal and opposite, which is what equal and opposite forces acting for the same time produce. A 48 kg pumpkin cart at 1.5 m/s carries 72, so the 36 kg straw cart would have to leave at 72 divided by 36, which is 2 m/s west. Partial: finding both products equal to 36 with the 2 m/s prediction not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Trials 1 and 2 hold the push at 48 N and halve the mass from 24 kg to 12 kg, and the acceleration doubles from 2 to 4, so less mass means more change in motion for the same force. Trials 1 and 3 hold the mass at 24 kg and double the push from 48 N to 96 N, and the acceleration doubles from 2 to 4. Trial 4 fits force equals mass times acceleration: 36 kg times 2 m/s each second is 72 N, the push listed. Partial: naming both pairs of trials with trial 4 not checked against force equals mass times acceleration is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]},{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying what changes a magnetic force, relating weight to mass, and naming the field in a gap",html:()=>`<p class="big">A magnet is held above a steel nail on the bench and the pull on the nail is measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gap</th><th>Pull with one magnet</th><th>Pull with two magnets stacked</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1 cm</td><td>1.80 N</td><td>3.50 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2 cm</td><td>0.45 N</td><td>0.88 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3 cm</td><td>0.20 N</td><td>0.39 N</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The same 25 kg pumpkin, weighed in three places.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Weight on the scale</th></tr>
     <tr><td>in the patch, at sea level</td><td>245.0 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on a mountain 4,400 m up</td><td>244.7 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on the moon</td><td>41 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("key",74)}${ico("compass",74)}${ico("pumpkin",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use the first chart. Name the two things that change the pull on the nail and give the numbers that show each.","Describe the number pattern down either column, and say what finding the same pattern in a different kind of force suggests about how forces reach across space.","Explain all three pumpkin weights, and name what occupies the gap between the magnet and the nail while they are not touching."],key:["The gap and the strength of the magnet. Gap: with one magnet the pull falls from 1.80 N at 1 cm to 0.45 N at 2 cm to 0.20 N at 3 cm. Magnet strength: stacking a second magnet raises the pull at every gap, 1.80 N to 3.50 N at 1 cm and 0.45 N to 0.88 N at 2 cm, roughly doubling it. Partial: naming both factors with the figures quoted for only one of them is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Doubling the gap from 1 cm to 2 cm cut the pull to about a quarter, and trebling it to 3 cm cut it to about a ninth, in both columns, so the pull falls off with the square of the distance. The electric force between charges and the gravitational force between masses fall off the same way, which suggests all three act through something spreading out from the source into the space around it rather than by reaching directly to the target. Partial: naming the squared fall off with nothing said about what the same pattern in gravity and electric force suggests is approaching. (MS-PS2)","The pumpkin holds the same 25 kg of matter in all three places. Weight is the gravitational force on it, which depends on its mass, the mass of the world, and the distance from that world's center. On the mountain the pumpkin is slightly farther from Earth's center, so the pull is slightly less, 245.0 N to 244.7 N. The moon has about an eightieth of Earth's mass, so it pulls the same pumpkin with only 41 N. The gap between magnet and nail is filled by a magnetic field, which is how the pull crosses empty space. Partial: explaining two of the three weights is approaching, and the small drop on the mountain is usually the one left out. (MS-PS2)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Shelf and Slope",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["MS-PS3"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating kinetic energy to mass and speed and stored energy to the arrangement of objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A loaded barrow is rolled down the slope at the edge of the patch and its kinetic energy is measured at the bottom.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>5 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>10 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>10 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>20 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>5 kg</td><td>6 m/s</td><td>90 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>20 kg</td><td>3 m/s</td><td>90 J</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Stored energy measured for a 12 kg pumpkin on the barn shelf, and for two magnets held facing each other so that they push apart.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Stored energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>pumpkin 0.6 m above the barn floor</td><td>70.6 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pumpkin 1.2 m above the barn floor</td><td>141.1 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pumpkin 2.4 m above the barn floor</td><td>282.2 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the two magnets held 1.0 cm apart</td><td>0.05 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the two magnets held 0.5 cm apart</td><td>0.10 J</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare trials 1 and 2, then 1 and 3. State how kinetic energy depends on mass and how it depends on speed.","Trials 3 and 4 both come to 90 J by different routes. Show how each gets there, and say which of mass and speed is the more powerful lever.","Use the pumpkin rows. State how stored energy depends on height, and explain what the height is measured from and why that choice matters.","The magnet rows store more energy as the gap closes and the pumpkin rows store more as the gap opens. Explain both with one idea."],key:["Trials 1 and 2: doubling the mass from 5 kg to 10 kg at the same 2 m/s doubled the energy from 10 J to 20 J, so at a fixed speed kinetic energy is proportional to mass. Trials 1 and 3: trebling the speed from 2 to 6 m/s multiplied the energy by nine, 10 J to 90 J, so kinetic energy goes up with the square of the speed. Partial: one of the two relationships stated with the figures is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Trial 3 keeps the 5 kg mass and trebles the speed, and three squared is nine, so 10 J becomes 90 J. Trial 4 keeps a modest 3 m/s but carries 20 kg, four times trial 1's mass, and one and a half times its speed, and 4 times 1.5 squared is 4 times 2.25, which is 9, so again 90 J. Speed is the stronger lever, because it is squared: doubling the speed multiplies the energy by four while doubling the mass only doubles it. Partial: showing both routes to 90 J with no judgment given on which lever is stronger is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Doubling the height doubled the stored energy, 70.6 J at 0.6 m and 141.1 J at 1.2 m, and four times the height gave four times the energy, 282.2 J at 2.4 m. The height is measured from the barn floor, which was chosen, not discovered. Measured from the yard outside, or from the loft above, the same pumpkin on the same shelf would carry a different number, because stored energy belongs to an arrangement of two objects and depends on the reference position named. Partial: describing the proportion to height with nothing said about the barn floor being a chosen reference is approaching. (MS-PS3)","In both cases energy is stored by moving objects against the force between them. The magnets push each other apart, so pressing them closer works against that push and stores energy, and letting go releases it. Earth pulls the pumpkin down, so lifting it works against that pull and stores energy, and letting go releases it. The store grows in whichever direction opposes the force, which is why one grows as the gap closes and the other as it opens. Partial: explaining one of the two stores with nothing said about both being work done against the force between the objects is approaching. (MS-PS3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Ripples on the Pond",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"relating amplitude to the energy in a wave and using wavelength and frequency to find wave speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">Ripples are made at one end of the irrigation pond. A small paddle wheel at the far end turns a little on each crest, and the turn is measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Ripple</th><th>Amplitude</th><th>Wavelength</th><th>Crests per second</th><th>Degrees the paddle turns on one crest</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>1 cm</td><td>24 cm</td><td>5</td><td>2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>2 cm</td><td>24 cm</td><td>5</td><td>8</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>3 cm</td><td>24 cm</td><td>5</td><td>18</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>2 cm</td><td>12 cm</td><td>10</td><td>8</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("drop",74)}${ico("wave",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare A, B and C. Describe how the paddle's turn depends on amplitude, and name the rule.","Compare B and D. Find the speed of each ripple and explain why the two come out the same.","Use the chart to explain what a wave carries across the pond, given that the water does not flow to the far end."],key:["Doubling the amplitude from 1 cm to 2 cm multiplied the turn by four, 2 degrees to 8 degrees, and trebling it to 3 cm multiplied the turn by nine, 2 degrees to 18 degrees. The energy a crest delivers goes up with the square of the amplitude, which is why amplitude is the measure of a wave's energy and the only column here that changes the turn. Partial: reading the bigger turn off the chart with nothing said about the energy going up with the square of the amplitude is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Speed is wavelength times crests per second. B: 0.24 m times 5 is 1.2 m per second. D: 0.12 m times 10 is also 1.2 m per second. The speed of a ripple is set by the water and its depth, not by how the ripple was made, so halving the wavelength forces the frequency to double. Both still deliver 8 degrees per crest, because the amplitude is unchanged. Partial: working out one of the two speeds, or both with nothing said about the water setting the speed, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Energy, and the pattern that carries it. The paddle at the far end turns, so something arrived with energy, and the amount depends on the amplitude. Yet each parcel of water only rises and falls about its own place and settles back, so nothing flows from one end to the other. A wave is a disturbance traveling through a medium, and it moves energy without moving the medium along with it. Partial: saying the energy crosses the pond, with nothing said about each parcel of water settling back in its own place, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]},{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Use the chart and the two methods. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading reflection, absorption and transmission from data and comparing a digital with an analog signal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp sends 100 units of light at each material. One meter sits behind it and one beside the lamp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Passed through</th><th>Came back</th><th>Neither</th></tr>
     <tr><td>pond water, 30 cm, clear</td><td>61</td><td>7</td><td>32</td></tr>
     <tr><td>pond water, 30 cm, silt stirred in</td><td>9</td><td>34</td><td>57</td></tr>
     <tr><td>glass pane</td><td>91</td><td>7</td><td>2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>white tarpaulin</td><td>2</td><td>78</td><td>20</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dark soil</td><td>0</td><td>7</td><td>93</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two ways to get the gate's visitor count to the farmhouse.<br>Method 1: a tone whose pitch rises with the count, matched by ear against a tuning fork.<br>Method 2: the count sent as short beeps in groups, each group standing for one digit.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("megaphone",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare the two water rows. Name which of the three outcomes the silt changed most, and say what the silt is doing to the light.","The glass passes 91 and the white tarpaulin returns 78. Explain why you can see through one and not the other.","A gust makes the sound carry unevenly. Say which method still delivers the right count and why, then name one thing the other method does better."],key:["Transmission changed most: 61 fell to 9, a drop of 52 units, while reflection rose 7 to 34 and absorption rose 32 to 57. The silt particles scatter light in all directions, sending much of it back out of the water or sideways, and they also absorb some of it, so far less reaches the far side. Cloudy water does not swallow light so much as redirect it. Partial: naming transmission as the biggest change with nothing said about the silt scattering the light rather than only swallowing it is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Glass transmits most of the light and transmits it straight through, so the pattern of light leaving the far side still matches the pattern that went in, and an image survives the trip. The tarpaulin sends 78 units back, and it sends them back scattered in every direction, so the pattern is destroyed even though plenty of light returns. Seeing through something needs light that passes through and keeps its direction, which is why the first column and not the brightness decides it. Partial: contrasting the 91 passed with the 78 returned, with nothing said about transmitted light having to keep its direction, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The grouped beeps. That is a digital signal: each beep only has to be heard or not heard, so an uneven gust still leaves the pattern readable and the count arrives exactly as sent. The rising pitch is analog, where the value is carried by the pitch itself, so anything that shifts how the tone is heard shifts the reported count with no sign of error. The analog method is quicker to send and can carry a value between whole steps, which the digital code cannot unless the code is extended. Partial: choosing the grouped beeps with nothing said about why the rising pitch hides its own error, or with nothing named that the analog method does better, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Out of the Mud",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Read the situation and the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and evaluating competing designs against them",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rain has turned the field track to mud and the loaded wagon sinks to its axles. Nine hundred pumpkins have to reach the stand 300 m away within three days, working six hours a day. The patch has $400, two people, and a gate 2 m wide that everything must pass through.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Cost</th><th>Build time</th><th>Pumpkins per hour</th><th>Width</th><th>Fruit damaged</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: lay a plank track</td><td>$150</td><td>5 hours</td><td>110</td><td>1.4 m</td><td>1 in 90</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: hire a tracked loader</td><td>$1,100</td><td>none</td><td>400</td><td>2.6 m</td><td>1 in 30</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: plastic sleds pulled by hand</td><td>$90</td><td>1 hour</td><td>55</td><td>0.9 m</td><td>1 in 120</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pumpkin",74)}${ico("truck",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["State this as a design problem, then sort the chart's columns into criteria and constraints.","Which design is ruled out, and on which constraints? Name every one it breaks.","Work out whether each remaining design can move 900 pumpkins in the time available, then choose one and defend it from the chart."],key:["Achieve: move 900 pumpkins 300 m across soft ground to the stand without ruining them. Criteria, the measures of success: pumpkins moved per hour and the share of fruit damaged. Constraints, the limits on any solution: no more than $400, finished within three days of six hours, only two people, and nothing wider than the 2 m gate. Rate and damage are the criteria; cost, time, labor and width are constraints. Partial: a goal stated with cost or the gate width filed as criteria rather than constraints is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Design B is out twice over: $1,100 breaks the $400 budget, and at 2.6 m it will not pass a 2 m gate. It is also the worst on damage, 1 in 30 against 1 in 90 and 1 in 120, so even its very high rate does not save it. A solution that breaks a constraint is out whatever else it does well. Partial: naming Design B with one of the two constraints it breaks is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Three days of six hours is 18 hours. A spends 5 hours building, leaving 13 hours of moving at 110 an hour, which is 1,430 pumpkins, comfortably over 900. C spends 1 hour, leaving 17 hours at 55 an hour, which is 935, over 900 by only 35. A is the better choice: it finishes with a wide margin, costs $150 of the $400, fits the gate, and damages 1 in 90. C is cheaper and gentler on the fruit but leaves no room at all if anything goes wrong, and with two people and no margin a single lost hour puts the job over. Partial: working out that both designs can reach 900 with no choice made and defended, or a choice defended with the hours not worked out, is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to find which change helps and planning the next round of testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four versions of the plank track were tried over the same 50 m of mud.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Version</th><th>Plank width</th><th>Rows of plank under the wheels</th><th>Pumpkins per load</th><th>Pumpkins per hour</th><th>Times the wagon stuck</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>20 cm</td><td>one</td><td>90</td><td>70</td><td>6</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>30 cm</td><td>one</td><td>90</td><td>104</td><td>2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>30 cm</td><td>two</td><td>90</td><td>110</td><td>0</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>30 cm</td><td>one</td><td>45</td><td>86</td><td>0</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("truck",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name the one change between versions 1 and 2 and between 2 and 3, and say what each did to the rate and to the sticking.","Version 4 halved the load and the sticking stopped, but the rate fell. Explain the trade, and say what the chart says to do instead.","Write version 5 and predict its result, then name a variable the chart has not tested and say how you would test it."],key:["Versions 1 to 2: only the plank width changed, 20 cm to 30 cm, and the rate rose from 70 to 104 an hour while the sticking fell from 6 times to 2. Versions 2 to 3: only the number of rows changed, one to two, and the rate rose slightly from 104 to 110 while the sticking fell from 2 to none. Each test changed one variable, which is the only reason a result can be assigned to a cause. Partial: naming both changes with the effect on the sticking not given alongside the rate is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","A lighter load presses less on the planks, so the wheels stop sinking and the sticking ends, but every load now carries half as many pumpkins, so twice as many trips are needed and the rate falls from 104 to 86. Version 3 reaches zero sticking by widening the support instead of lightening the load, and keeps the full 90 per load, so it gets both: 110 an hour and no sticking. The chart says to spread the load rather than reduce it. Partial: explaining the trade with nothing said about version 3 getting both by spreading the load instead is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any version argued from the chart. The strongest keeps 30 cm planks in two rows and the full 90 per load, which is version 3, and tests whether a wider plank still, say 40 cm in one row, matches two rows of 30 cm for less wood; predict about 110 an hour and no sticking. The untested variable is the plank material and surface, since a slippery or splintered plank changes both rate and damage, so the next test should hold width, rows and load at version 3's settings and compare two or three plank surfaces. Partial: a version 5 argued from the chart with no untested variable named and no test for it described is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Two Wants",std:["RL.9-10.3"],needs:["RL.8.3"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Emeka wants two things that pull against each other. Name both. Give a detail from the story for each one.</li><li>How does the choice he makes at the end show the message of the story?</li></ol>${story()}`,key:["He wants to win after five months of work (he fed, watered, and shaded the pumpkin, and it is the heaviest of the day). He also wants to be fair and honest (“If we hide it and win, who comes in second?”). Both wants and a detail for each. (HS RL.9-10.3, a character with conflicting motivations)","He gives up the prize to stay honest, which shows the theme: how you win matters more than winning, or a win you did not earn is not worth having. The judge’s respect and the picture he takes “anyway” support it. (HS RL.9-10.3, how a character’s choices develop the theme)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Fraction Exponents",std:["N-RN.2"],needs:["8.EE.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Rewrite 25<sup>1/2</sup> using a root symbol. Then find its value.</li><li>Find the value of 27<sup>1/3</sup>.</li><li>Find the value of 4<sup>3/2</sup>.</li></ol>`,key:["√25 = 5 (HS N-RN.2)","3, because ∛27 = 3 (HS N-RN.2)","8, because √4 = 2 and 2<sup>3</sup> = 8 (HS N-RN.2)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Roots at Work",std:["A-REI.4"],needs:["8.EE.A.2"],n:2,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Give every solution.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Solve 2x<sup>2</sup> = 72.</li><li>Solve (x − 3)<sup>2</sup> = 25.</li></ol>`,key:["x = 6 and x = −6. Divide by 2 to get x<sup>2</sup> = 36, then take square roots. (HS A-REI.4)","x = 8 and x = −2. Take square roots: x − 3 = 5 or x − 3 = −5. (HS A-REI.4)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","RL.9-10.3"];const WRITE_STD=["W.8.1","W.8.2","W.8.3"];const MATH_STD=["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","N-RN.2","A-REI.4"];const LOG_NAMES={readq:"Questions read aloud",passage:"Text read aloud by an adult",repeat:"Directions repeated or reworded",vocab:"Word or phrase explained by teacher",hint:"Hint or first step given",fewer:"Fewer questions",time:"Extra time",scribe:"Adult wrote the student’s words",brk:"Break"};function stopLogs(st){const out=["readq"];if(READ_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("passage");out.push("repeat","vocab","hint","fewer","time");if(WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("scribe");out.push("brk");return out}const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function wt(st){return st.std.includes("W.8.8")?"notes":st.std.includes("W.8.1")?"argument":st.std.includes("W.8.3")?"narrative":"informative"}const G_STORY=[["pallet","a flat wooden platform used to move heavy loads"],["fairgrounds","the place where a fair is held"],["forklift","a small truck with two metal arms that lifts heavy loads"],["stem","the stalk that joins a pumpkin to its vine"],["rot","soft, spoiled places on a fruit"],["display","to show something"],["coach","a fancy carriage pulled by horses"]];const G_TON=[["variety","one named kind of plant"],["guarantee","to promise that something will happen"],["improves the odds","makes something more likely"],["vine","the long stem of a pumpkin plant that runs along the ground"],["sprout","to begin to grow"],["swells","gets bigger"],["sag","to sink or droop"]];const G_SRC=[["GPS","a system that uses satellites to show an exact location"],["receiver","a device that picks up a signal"],["digital map","a map stored on a computer"],["forum","a website where anyone can post messages"]];const G_WRITE={informative:[["rind","the tough outer skin of a pumpkin"],["clippers","a cutting tool like strong scissors"],["curing","leaving a crop out to dry and harden"],["bin","a large box"]],argument:[["weigh-off","a contest to find the heaviest pumpkin"],["expected","likely to happen"],["risk","something that could go wrong"]]};function glossHtml(st){if(st.id===13)return dl(G_WRITE[wt(st)]||G_WRITE.informative);return dl({10:G_TON,12:G_SRC}[st.id]||G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["strongest evidence","the words that prove the answer best, not just words on the same topic"],["explicit","stated right in the text"],["inference","something the text does not say outright, but that you can figure out from clues"],["dialogue","the words characters say out loud"],["incident","one event in a story"],["reveal","to show something that was hidden"],["figurative language","words that mean something beyond their plain meaning, like a comparison"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its dictionary meaning"],["tone","the feeling or attitude the words create"],["allusion","a quick reference to a well-known story, person, or event"],["theme","the message or big idea of a story"]];const FRAMES={cite:["The answer is ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____.","I can infer that ____ because the text says “____.”"],"RL.8.3":["When ____ says “____,” it makes ____ decide to ____.","This line shows that ____ is ____ because ____.","Because ____ happened, ____."],"RL.8.4":["The author compares ____ to ____. This shows ____.","The word ____ makes me feel ____.","The tone is ____ because of the words “____.”"]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.includes("RL.8.3")||st.std.includes("RL.9-10.3")?"RL.8.3":st.std.includes("RL.8.4")?"RL.8.4":"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FRAMES[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function verbsHtml(){return`<h4>Verbals: verb forms doing another job</h4>`+dl([["Gerund","ends in -ing and acts as a noun. <em>Hiking is fun.</em>"],["Participle","often ends in -ing or -ed and describes a noun. <em>The barking dog ran.</em>"],["Infinitive","to + verb. <em>She likes to draw.</em>"]])+`<h4>Voice</h4>`+dl([["Active","the subject does the action. <em>Ray kicked the ball.</em>"],["Passive","the subject receives the action. <em>The ball was kicked by Ray.</em>"]])+`<h4>Mood</h4>`+dl([["Indicative","states a fact. <em>It is raining.</em>"],["Imperative","gives a command. <em>Open the window.</em>"],["Interrogative","asks a question. <em>Is it raining?</em>"],["Conditional","tells what would or could happen. <em>I would go if I could.</em>"],["Subjunctive","a wish or something not true. Uses were. <em>If she were taller, she could reach.</em>"]])+`<p class="small">Keep the same voice and the same mood all the way through a sentence.</p>`}const PLAN={argument:["My claim","Reason 1 and a fact from the chart","Reason 2 and a fact from the chart","The other plan’s best point, and my answer","Conclusion"],informative:["The topic: what I will explain","Step or fact 1","Step or fact 2","Step or fact 3","Conclusion: why it matters"],narrative:["Who, where, and when","The problem","What happened first, next, last","A line of dialogue I will use","How it ended and what it meant"]};function planHtml(st){return`<p class="small">Copy these headings onto your paper. Jot two or three words next to each one before you start.</p><ol class="card-list">${(PLAN[wt(st)]||PLAN.informative).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`}const SENT={argument:{s:["The farm should choose ____ because ____.","The chart shows that ____.","Some people might choose ____ because ____. However, ____.","As a result, ____.","For these reasons, ____."],w:["because","for example","however","although","as a result","for these reasons"]},informative:{s:["A pumpkin goes through several steps before it reaches a store.","First, ____.","After that, ____.","This is called ____.","As a result, ____.","In the end, ____."],w:["first","after that","because","as a result","for example","in the end"]},narrative:{s:["It was ____ when ____.","At first, ____.","“____,” ____ said.","By the time ____, ____.","Looking back, I realized ____."],w:["at first","a moment later","suddenly","after that","by the time","finally","looking back"]},notes:{s:["I trust Source ____ more because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Elena Ruiz writes, “____.”","According to Elena Ruiz, ____.","Author. “Title.” Magazine, date."],w:["according to","writes","states","explains"]}};function sentHtml(st){const d=SENT[wt(st)];return`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}const CHECK={argument:["I stated my claim.","I gave reasons with facts from the chart.","I named the other plan’s best point and answered it.","I used linking words.","I kept a formal style (no slang).","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],informative:["I introduced the topic.","My steps are in an order that makes sense.","I explained the facts in my own sentences.","I used linking words.","I used topic words.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],narrative:["I set the scene.","Events are in an order that makes sense.","I used dialogue.","I used details the reader can see, hear, or feel.","I used time words.","My ending reflects on what happened.","I reread it for mistakes."]};function checkHtml(st){return`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${(CHECK[wt(st)]||CHECK.informative).map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/\u00D7/g,"*").replace(/\u00F7/g,"/").replace(/\u2212/g,"-");if(!e||!/^[0-9+\-*/().\s]*$/.test(e))return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","C","+","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Add, subtract, multiply, and divide. To see a fraction as a decimal, divide the top by the bottom. This calculator has no power or root keys.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+\u2212\u00D7\u00F7]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function sqHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>n</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=12;n++)h+=`<th>${n}</th>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n²</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=12;n++)h+=`<td>${n*n}</td>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n³</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=12;n++)h+=`<td>${n<=6?n*n*n:""}</td>`;return h+`</tr></table></div><p class="small">n² means n × n. n³ means n × n × n. To find a square root, find the number in the n² row and look up to n. To find a cube root, use the n³ row.</p>`}const rules=rows=>`<table class="meas">${rows.map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")}</table>`;const FORM={"8.NS.A.1":["Rational and irrational",ul(["<strong>Rational:</strong> can be written as a fraction of two integers. Its decimal ends or repeats.","<strong>Irrational:</strong> cannot be written as a fraction. Its decimal never ends and never repeats.","<strong>Repeating decimal to fraction:</strong> call it x. If one digit repeats, multiply by 10. If two digits repeat, multiply by 100. Subtract so the repeating part cancels, then divide."])],"8.EE.A.1":["Exponent rules",rules([["Multiply, same base: add the exponents","a<sup>m</sup> × a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m+n</sup>"],["Divide, same base: subtract the exponents","a<sup>m</sup> ÷ a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m−n</sup>"],["Power of a power: multiply the exponents","(a<sup>m</sup>)<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>mn</sup>"],["Zero exponent","a<sup>0</sup> = 1"],["Negative exponent: flip it to a fraction","a<sup>−n</sup> = 1/a<sup>n</sup>"]])],"8.EE.A.2":["Roots",ul(["If x<sup>2</sup> = p, then x = √p or x = −√p.","If x<sup>3</sup> = p, then x = ∛p.","√ means “what number times itself gives this?”","∛ means “what number used as a factor three times gives this?”"])],"8.G.B.7":["Right triangles",rules([["Pythagorean theorem","a² + b² = c²"]])+ul(["c is always the longest side, the one across from the right angle.","To find a leg, subtract: a² = c² − b².","Finish by taking the square root."])],"8.F.A.3":["Linear or not",ul(["A linear function can be written y = mx + b. Its graph is a straight line.","m is the rate of change. In a linear function the output changes by the same amount for each step in the input.","y = x² is not linear: equal steps in x give unequal steps in y."])],"8.G.A.3":["Transformation rules",rules([["Translate right a, up b","(x, y) → (x + a, y + b)"],["Reflect across the x-axis","(x, y) → (x, −y)"],["Reflect across the y-axis","(x, y) → (−x, y)"],["Rotate 90° counterclockwise about the origin","(x, y) → (−y, x)"],["Rotate 90° clockwise about the origin","(x, y) → (y, −x)"],["Rotate 180° about the origin","(x, y) → (−x, −y)"],["Dilate by scale factor k from the origin","(x, y) → (kx, ky)"]])+`<p class="small">Left and down are negative moves.</p>`]};function formHtml(st){return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`).join("")}const EX={"8.NS.A.1":[["Turn 0.1333... into a fraction (only the 3 repeats)",["Let x = 0.1333...","Multiply by 10: 10x = 1.333...","Multiply by 100: 100x = 13.333...","Subtract: 100x − 10x = 13.333... − 1.333..., so 90x = 12","Divide and simplify: x = 12/90 = 2/15"]]],"8.EE.A.1":[["Simplify 7<sup>2</sup> × 7<sup>−6</sup>",["The bases match, so add the exponents: 2 + (−6) = −4","7<sup>2</sup> × 7<sup>−6</sup> = 7<sup>−4</sup>","A negative exponent flips to a fraction: 7<sup>−4</sup> = 1/7<sup>4</sup>"]]],"8.EE.A.2":[["Solve x<sup>2</sup> = 64 and x<sup>3</sup> = 64",["x<sup>2</sup> = 64: what number times itself is 64? 8 × 8 = 64, and (−8) × (−8) = 64. So x = 8 or x = −8.","x<sup>3</sup> = 64: what number used three times is 64? 4 × 4 × 4 = 64. So x = 4.","(−4) × (−4) × (−4) = −64, so −4 does not work."]]],"8.G.A.3":[["Move the point (−4, 2) four ways",["Translate with (x + 5, y − 3): add 5 to x and subtract 3 from y. (1, −1)","Reflect across the y-axis: change the sign of x. (4, 2)","Rotate 90° clockwise about the origin: (x, y) → (y, −x). (2, 4)","Dilate by a scale factor of 1/2: multiply both by 1/2. (−2, 1)"]]]};function exHtml(st){return st.std.filter(x=>EX[x]).map(x=>EX[x].map(e=>`<h4>${e[0]}</h4><ol class="card-list">${e[1].map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`).join("")).join("")+`<p class="small">These are different problems from the ones on the stop. Use the same steps.</p>`}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;const GS=440,GM=10,GU=(GS-68)/(2*GM);function pgridHtml(){const lines=[];if(gridJoin)for(let i=1;i<gridPts.length;i++)lines.push([gridPts[i-1],gridPts[i]]);return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div><div class="figure" data-pg style="cursor:crosshair">${F.coordGrid({max:GM,quad4:true,size:GS,points:gridPts,lines})}</div><p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const svg=b.querySelector("[data-pg] svg");if(!svg)return;svg.onclick=e=>{const q=svg.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*GS/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*GS/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/GU)-GM,y=Math.round((GS-34-vy)/GU)-GM;if(Math.abs(x)>GM||Math.abs(y)>GM)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help",std:["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","W.8.8","W.8.1","W.8.2","RL.9-10.3"],html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD,html:framesHtml},verbs:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.8.1"],html:verbsHtml},plan:{name:"Planner",std:WRITE_STD,html:planHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.8.8"].concat(WRITE_STD),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},sq:{name:"Squares and cubes chart",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.2","N-RN.2","A-REI.4"],html:sqHtml},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},ex:{name:"Worked example",std:Object.keys(EX),html:exHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["8.G.A.3"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"terms",std:["RL.8.4","RL.8.3"],msg:"The reading terms card defines words like tone, connotation, and allusion. This stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.B.7"],msg:"The card states the Pythagorean theorem and how to find a leg, so these two items show applying a given formula, not recalling it."},{t:"form",std:["8.F.A.3"],msg:"The card says what makes a function linear, which is most of what this item asks. Count it as supported, not independent."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1"],msg:"The calculator did the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set the problem up correctly and said what the numbers mean."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and thinking the student supplied."},{t:"verbs",std:["L.8.1"],msg:"The grammar card defines verbals, voice, and mood with examples, so this stop shows using a reference, not recall."},{t:"plan",msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"calc",std:["8.NS.A.1"],msg:"The calculator shows the decimal for a fraction, so count the fraction to decimal question as supported."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2"],msg:"The calculator can find a value by repeated multiplication. Rate on whether the student used the exponent rule or the root, not only the value."},{t:"sq",std:["8.EE.A.2"],msg:"The chart gives square roots and cube roots, so count the “find the value” question as supported."},{t:"sq",std:["8.NS.A.1"],msg:"The chart shows that 81 is a perfect square and 50 is not, so count the sorting question as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.NS.A.1"],msg:"The card gives the definitions and the steps for repeating decimals, so this stop shows applying them with a reference, not recall."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.A.1"],msg:"With the exponent rules on the card, this stop shows using the rules, not remembering them."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.A.2"],msg:"The card tells how many solutions each kind of equation has, so count “give every solution” as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.A.3"],msg:"The card gives the coordinate rule for each move, so this stop shows applying given rules, not describing the effect independently."},{t:"ex",msg:"A worked example models the method. Count the matching questions as supported."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.A.3"],msg:"If points were plotted on the grid tool, note whether the student found the coordinates by plotting or by using a rule."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult wrote, rate the ideas and organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,logNames:LOG_NAMES,stopLogs}}});
